WEBVTT

00:00:02.370 --> 00:00:14.530
<v Ira Glass>Kristy went to one of those high schools for the performing arts, like in Fame , except not good. One of her teachers would leave the room for long stretches to smoke cigarettes, another spent lots of class time gossiping about which celebrities were dating.
<v Ira Glass>克里斯蒂上了一所专门培养表演艺术的高中，就像电影《名扬四海》里那样，不过水平没那么好。她的老师之一经常长时间离开教室去抽烟，另一位则把大部分课堂时间都用来八卦哪个明星在谈恋爱。

00:00:14.530 --> 00:00:35.890
<v Kristy Kruger>One of my music teachers, he never got the memo about separation of church and state. He would talk about Jesus all the time. And he actually would ask, "So, raise your hands. How many of you went to church on Sunday? I bet you would have gone to church if I would have given you extra credit."
<v Kristy Kruger>我的音乐老师之一，他从没听过“政教分离”这回事。他总是谈论耶稣。他甚至会问：“好，举手。周日去教堂的人有多少？我敢说如果我给你们平时分，你们肯定会去教堂的。”

00:00:35.890 --> 00:00:50.940
<v Ira Glass>When a jazz piano class conflicted with World History, her counselor simply signed her out of World History. He listed it on her transcript as a class that she took, but she never actually went to class. It was that kind of school.
<v Ira Glass>当爵士钢琴课和世界历史课冲突时，她的辅导员直接给她退了世界历史课。他在她的成绩单上列出这门课，但她实际上从来没去上过。那所学校就是这样。

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:57.620
<v Ira Glass>She was there to study piano, and that's what she devoted all of her energy to. She practiced after school for four hours to eight hours a day.
<v Ira Glass>她在那儿是为了学习钢琴，她把所有的精力都投入到了这件事上。她每天放学后练习四到八个小时。

00:00:57.620 --> 00:01:11.060
<v Kristy Kruger>Yeah, so I practiced a lot. I mean, I didn't really know much outside the walls of the practice room. And I just really realized, when I got to college, I was way behind. I mean, I didn't even know how to write a paper, you know?
<v Kristy Kruger>是的，所以我练习了很多。我是说，除了琴房，我对外面世界了解得并不多。当我上大学时，我才真正意识到，我落后太多了。我是说，我甚至不知道该怎么写论文，你知道吗？

00:01:11.060 --> 00:01:27.120
<v Ira Glass>But it wasn't exactly in class in college that she realized just how far behind she was. She could fake it in class. It was on stage that she realized, in an improvisational comedy troupe that she joined as a freshman. On stage, her lack of knowledge could be kind of harrowing.
<v Ira Glass>但她在大学里意识到自己落后这么多，其实并不是在课堂上。她在课堂上还能伪装一下。直到她大一加入了一个即兴喜剧团，在舞台上她才意识到这一点。在舞台上，她的无知可能会变得相当可怕。

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:40.590
<v Kristy Kruger>Like, if someone referred to Henry Kissinger in a scene, I'd just have no idea who that was, at all. The audience would throw out suggestions, like the Hindenburg thing.
<v Kristy Kruger>比如，如果有人在场景中提到亨利·基辛格，我完全不知道那是谁。观众会提出建议，比如兴登堡号空难之类的事。

00:01:40.590 --> 00:01:41.570
<v Kristy Kruger>[LAUGHS]
<v Kristy Kruger>[笑声]

00:01:41.570 --> 00:01:43.550
<v Ira Glass>The Hindenburg disaster, yeah.
<v Ira Glass>兴登堡号灾难，是的。

00:01:43.550 --> 00:01:49.460
<v Kristy Kruger>Yeah, and I just had no idea.
<v Kristy Kruger>是的，我完全不知道。

00:01:49.460 --> 00:01:54.370
<v Ira Glass>And then you had to re-enact it on a stage in front of people, and you had no idea what it referred to?
<v Ira Glass>然后你不得不在观众面前的舞台上重现它，而你根本不知道它指的是什么？

00:01:54.370 --> 00:02:21.510
<v Kristy Kruger>I cannot tell you how many times something like that happened, where everybody was laughing and I had no idea why they were laughing. It was really scary, actually, to be honest. I mean, I was so nervous. And I really wanted to stay in the troupe. I didn't want to get kicked out, but I really was so clueless.
<v Kristy Kruger>我无法形容这种事发生了多少次，大家都笑成一片，而我根本不知道他们在笑什么。说实话，那真的很可怕。我是说，我当时太紧张了。我真的很想留在剧团里。我不想被踢出去，但我确实太无知了。

00:02:21.510 --> 00:02:41.510
<v Ira Glass>In one of the sketches they would do on stage, they would ask the audience for a bunch of historical events, and then they would do their own little renditions of these events in the historical order that they happened. And in performing this one day, Kristy confounded her cast mates by putting World War I before the Salem witch trials, but she did manage to locate it after the invention of the wheel.
<v Ira Glass>在他们舞台上的一个小品里，他们会向观众索取一堆历史事件，然后按发生的先后顺序表演这些事件。有一天在表演时，克里斯蒂把第一次世界大战排在了塞勒姆审巫案之前，让她的搭档们大惑不解，但她确实设法把它排在了车轮发明之后。

00:02:41.510 --> 00:03:04.490
<v Kristy Kruger>And after the whole show, everybody was like, "God, Kristy. What was up with that? I mean, why did you start off with World War I? It was the Salem witch trials." They just thought that I wasn't thinking. And I was like, "I didn't know that World War I came after the Salem witch trials."
<v Kristy Kruger>演出结束后，大家都说：“天哪，克里斯蒂。那是怎么回事？我是说，你为什么要从第一次世界大战开始？那是塞勒姆审巫案啊。”他们只是以为我没过脑子。我说：“我不知道一战是在塞勒姆审巫案之后发生的。”

00:03:04.490 --> 00:03:05.850
<v Kristy Kruger>[LAUGHS]
<v Kristy Kruger>[笑声]

00:03:05.850 --> 00:03:09.750
<v Kristy Kruger>Yeah. They kind of looked at me in disbelief.
<v Kristy Kruger>是的，他们看着我的眼神里充满了难以置信。

00:03:09.750 --> 00:03:12.970
<v Ira Glass>Did you know what the Salem witch trials were?
<v Ira Glass>你知道塞勒姆审巫案是什么吗？

00:03:12.970 --> 00:03:27.840
<v Kristy Kruger>I did. You know what, honestly? You know, I don't even think it's about that. I think it's about that I didn't remember anything about World War I. I thought if it was World War I, it must have happened a long time ago.
<v Kristy Kruger>我知道。说实话，你知道吗？我觉得重点根本不在这儿。我觉得重点在于我对一战没有任何印象。我心想如果是“第一次世界大战”，那它肯定发生在很久很久以前。

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:29.710
<v Kristy Kruger>[LAUGHTER]
<v Kristy Kruger>[笑声]

00:03:29.710 --> 00:03:30.650
<v Kristy Kruger>Does that make sense?
<v Kristy Kruger>这说得通吗？

00:03:30.650 --> 00:03:34.880
<v Ira Glass>Yeah. Because the world is so--
<v Ira Glass>是的。因为世界是如此……

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:37.840
<v Kristy Kruger>Because it's the first war.
<v Kristy Kruger>因为它是有史以来的第一场战争。

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:41.940
<v Ira Glass>That ever happened. Sure. Absolutely.
<v Ira Glass>确实。绝对是。

00:03:41.940 --> 00:03:45.950
<v Kristy Kruger>It was like, well, invention of the wheel, then it must have been World War I.
<v Kristy Kruger>当时我就想，好吧，那是车轮发明之后，那肯定就是第一次世界大战。

00:03:45.950 --> 00:03:46.300
<v Ira Glass>Right.
<v Ira Glass>对。

00:03:46.300 --> 00:03:48.680
<v Kristy Kruger>I had no idea it happened in the 1900s, you know?
<v Kristy Kruger>我根本不知道它是发生在20世纪，你知道吗？

00:03:48.680 --> 00:03:50.630
<v Kristy Kruger>[LAUGHTER]
<v Kristy Kruger>[笑声]

00:03:50.630 --> 00:03:52.580
<v Kristy Kruger>It was the first one.
<v Kristy Kruger>那是第一次。

00:03:52.580 --> 00:04:02.590
<v Ira Glass>Well, I can see the confusion.
<v Ira Glass>嗯，我可以理解这种困惑。

00:04:02.590 --> 00:04:13.980
<v Ira Glass>So, to cope with this problem you were having, that you were on stage, you have no idea what's going on, people are laughing at you, and you have no idea why, did you come up with any kind of coping strategy?
<v Ira Glass>那么，为了应对这个问题，当你在舞台上，你完全不知道发生了什么，人们在嘲笑你，而你却不知道为什么，你有没有想出什么应对策略？

00:04:13.980 --> 00:04:46.760
<v Kristy Kruger>I totally did. I came up with some characters that, in their essence, it was OK to be ignorant. So I would use a stoner a lot of times. I'd be like, "Whoa, dude. I have no idea what's going on." And then I would use the troll, which was really my favorite. I would just kind of throw my hair in my face and talk in gibberish. And I'd just be like, Dar! Flar! Figgy floo! Vee floogle ooh! Flogalo vase!, like that.
<v Kristy Kruger>我完全想到了。我设计了一些角色，本质上就是可以表现得无知。所以我经常扮演一个瘾君子。我会说：“哇，老兄。我根本不知道发生了什么。”然后我会用那个“巨怪”角色，那真是我的最爱。我会把头发遮在脸上，胡言乱语。我会说：“哒！夫拉！菲吉夫露！微夫露果呜！夫洛加洛花瓶！”就像那样。

00:04:46.760 --> 00:04:52.310
<v Ira Glass>You'd be in the middle of a scene with another performer, and suddenly that's what you would do? You would just get a stumper?
<v Ira Glass>你会在和另一个表演者演对手戏时突然这么做吗？你会遇到卡壳的时候吗？

00:04:52.310 --> 00:04:56.940
<v Kristy Kruger>Yeah, in every scene, regardless of the time period or setting.
<v Kristy Kruger>是的，在每一场戏里，不管时代背景或场景是什么。

00:04:56.940 --> 00:04:58.850
<v Ira Glass>Does that work?
<v Ira Glass>那管用吗？

00:04:58.850 --> 00:05:00.080
<v Kristy Kruger>Yeah. It works.
<v Kristy Kruger>是的。管用。

00:05:00.080 --> 00:05:06.130
<v Kristy Kruger>[LAUGHS]
<v Kristy Kruger>[笑声]

00:05:06.130 --> 00:05:16.020
<v Ira Glass>And that's how Kristy turned things around for herself, by pretending to be exactly the thing she was scared she was: an ignorant babbler.
<v Ira Glass>这就是克里斯蒂扭转局面的方式，通过假装成为她最害怕的样子：一个无知的胡言乱语者。

00:05:16.020 --> 00:05:22.510
<v Kristy Kruger>I think that it saved me. I think I was really lost in this troupe.
<v Kristy Kruger>我觉得这拯救了我。我觉得我在这个剧团里真的很迷茫。

00:05:22.510 --> 00:05:37.170
<v Ira Glass>Did you get into social situations that were awkward in exactly the same way that being on stage was awkward, where people would be talking about stuff and you wouldn't know what the hell is going on, and then, basically, you would just turn into the troll?
<v Ira Glass>你有没有在现实生活中遇到过和舞台上一样尴尬的情况，人们在谈论某些事情，你根本不知道发生了什么，然后你基本上就变成了那个巨怪？

00:05:37.170 --> 00:05:52.300
<v Kristy Kruger>I wish that the troll would work in real-life situations, where you're sitting around having a conversation and someone brings up a historic reference that I have no clue, and everyone else is engaged in this, and I just kind of wish I could contribute by going, "Well, fig, floo! Thar! Flar! Dar, de, var, far."
<v Kristy Kruger>我真希望在现实生活中也能用那套巨怪的把戏，当你坐在一起聊天，有人提到了一个我完全不了解的历史背景，而其他人都参与其中时，我真希望自己能通过说“嗯，菲吉，夫露！扎！夫拉！哒，得，瓦，发”来加入讨论。

00:05:52.300 --> 00:05:54.730
<v Kristy Kruger>[LAUGHTER]
<v Kristy Kruger>[笑声]

00:05:54.730 --> 00:05:59.300
<v Kristy Kruger>But, it doesn't really work out that way.
<v Kristy Kruger>但是，现实生活中确实行不通。

00:05:59.300 --> 00:06:10.290
<v Ira Glass>Well, today, on our radio show, we have three stories of people in the same situation Kristy was in, where people think of them as up for grabs. They're worried about how people will see them, and like Kristy--
<v Ira Glass>那么，今天在我们的广播节目中，我们要讲三个人的故事，他们处于和克里斯蒂一样的境地，人们觉得他们可以随意对待。他们很担心别人会怎么看他们，就像克里斯蒂一样……

00:06:10.290 --> 00:06:11.310
<v Kristy Kruger>Flogalo vase!
<v Kristy Kruger>夫洛加洛花瓶！

00:06:11.310 --> 00:06:17.230
<v Ira Glass>--they take action. They take control of how they're going to be seen with dramatic, drastic steps.
<v Ira Glass>……他们采取了行动。他们通过戏剧性和激进的措施，掌控了自己被他人看待的方式。

00:06:17.230 --> 00:06:19.170
<v Kristy Kruger>Fig! Floo! Thar! Flar! Far!
<v Kristy Kruger>菲吉！夫露！扎！夫拉！发！

00:06:19.170 --> 00:06:19.880
<v Ira Glass>Well said.
<v Ira Glass>说得好。

00:06:19.880 --> 00:06:21.370
<v Kristy Kruger>Flar! Dar, de, var, far!
<v Kristy Kruger>夫拉！哒，得，瓦，发！

00:06:21.370 --> 00:07:14.480
<v Ira Glass>From WBEZ, Chicago, it's This American Life , distributed by Public Radio International. I'm Ira Glass. Act One of our show today, Dewey Decibel System. In that story, an American institution calls in the cavalry, calls in very unlikely allies in order to change its own image. Act Two, Goldstein On Goldstein. In that act, Mr. Jonathan Goldstein considers the question of manliness, specifically, his own manliness, and turns to a real man for answers, namely, his dad. Act Three, Heart Shaped Box. In that act, a mom decides that she's going to remake all of the ideas that her son has about his father using a very simple tactic. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>这里是来自芝加哥WBEZ电台的《美国生活》，由公共广播国际公司发行。我是艾拉·格拉斯。我们今天节目的第一幕，“杜威十进制系统”。在这个故事中，一个美国机构召集了援军，寻求一些不太可能的盟友来改变自己的形象。第二幕，“戈德斯坦论戈德斯坦”。在那一幕里，乔纳森·戈德斯坦先生探讨了男子气概的问题，特别是他自己的男子气概，并向一个真正的男人寻求答案，也就是他的父亲。第三幕，“心形盒”。在那一幕里，一位母亲决定用一种非常简单的策略来重塑她儿子对他父亲的所有印象。请继续收听。

00:07:14.480 --> 00:07:31.710
<v Ira Glass>Act One. This first story is about an audacious act of rebranding done by a group of people who are not normally thought of as being very audacious at all: public librarians, in this case, teen librarians, the ones who work with teenagers. Alex Blumberg prepared this report last summer.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕。这个第一个故事是关于一群通常被认为一点也不大胆的人所做出的大胆重塑形象的行为：公共图书馆员，在这个案例中是青少年图书馆员，那些与青少年一起工作的人。亚历克斯·布伦伯格去年夏天准备了这份报道。

00:07:31.710 --> 00:07:57.650
<v Alex Blumberg>If you travel among the teen librarians of Michigan, as I did recently, you'll hear a strange phrase over and over again. The phrase is lock-in, like, did you hear how the lock-in went the other night in Kalamazoo? One of the biggest practitioners of the lock-in is a guy named Bill Harmer, the only person I've ever met who could accurately be described as a maverick teen librarian. He explained to me that a lock-in is when the library reopens its doors after closing time, lets a bunch of teenagers in, and then--
<v Alex Blumberg>如果你像我最近一样在密歇根州的青少年图书馆员中走动，你会一遍又一遍地听到一个奇怪的短语。这个短语是“闭馆留宿”，就像，你听说前几天卡拉马祖的闭馆留宿进行得怎么样了吗？“闭馆留宿”最大的实践者之一是一个叫比尔·哈默的家伙，他是唯一一个我见过的能被准确描述为“特立独行的青少年图书馆员”的人。他向我解释说，“闭馆留宿”是指图书馆在闭馆时间后重新打开大门，放进一群青少年，然后……

00:07:57.650 --> 00:08:00.240
<v Bill Harmer>Once they come in to the building, they can't leave.
<v Bill Harmer>一旦他们进入大楼，他们就不能离开了。

00:08:00.240 --> 00:08:00.840
<v Alex Blumberg>Oh, OK.
<v Alex Blumberg>哦，好的。

00:08:00.840 --> 00:08:04.900
<v Bill Harmer>So they're stuck there until the program's over.
<v Bill Harmer>所以他们得待在那里直到活动结束。

00:08:04.900 --> 00:08:10.680
<v Alex Blumberg>So inviting a bunch of teenagers to a library and then locking them in is a fun activity for them?
<v Alex Blumberg>所以邀请一群青少年去图书馆然后把他们锁在里面对他们来说是一项有趣的活动？

00:08:10.680 --> 00:08:24.140
<v Bill Harmer>Yeah. They love it. They love it. Every time I've done one, we've had at least 50 or more teenagers show up.
<v Bill Harmer>是的。他们喜欢。他们很喜欢。我每次办这个活动，都有至少50个或更多的青少年出现。

00:08:24.140 --> 00:08:41.810
<v Alex Blumberg>It turns out, the lock-in is a state-of-the-art technique in the arsenal of the modern, teen librarian. The library has an image problem. Teenagers see it as the equivalent of summer school: boring, no fun. But Bill Harmer and his colleagues say that's the old library, and they're on a campaign to change people's perceptions.
<v Alex Blumberg>事实证明，“闭馆留宿”是现代青少年图书馆员库中最先进的技巧。图书馆有一个形象问题。青少年把它看作是暑期学校的同义词：无聊，没意思。但比尔·哈默和他的同事们说，那是旧的图书馆，他们正在发起一场改变人们观念的运动。

00:08:41.810 --> 00:09:00.740
<v Alex Blumberg>Bill has attacked this problem with inspiring devotion. He's had sleep-overs in the library where 50 kids camped out between the stacks. He's hosted late night car tournaments. He stocks the teen section with the latest in Japanese Manga comics and graphic novels. And he's assembled the largest and most comprehensive DVD collection in the entire state, he says, including video stores.
<v Alex Blumberg>比尔以令人振奋的投入解决了这个问题。他举办过图书馆过夜活动，有50个孩子在书架间露营。他举办过深夜游戏锦标赛。他在青少年区备齐了最新的日本漫画和图像小说。他说，他组建了全州最庞大、最全面的DVD收藏，包括视频租赁店。

00:09:00.740 --> 00:09:20.460
<v Alex Blumberg>But last fall, Bill hatched a plan to take teen library programming to a place it's never gone before, to leave the lock-in in the dust. The plan was this, stage a series of concerts inside libraries all over Michigan, but not the type of music normally associated with the library: acoustic instruments, lyrics about ducks and bunnies, singing puppets.
<v Alex Blumberg>但去年秋天，比尔想出了一个计划，要把青少年图书馆活动带到一个前所未有的高度，把“闭馆留宿”远远甩在身后。计划就是：在密歇根州各地的图书馆内举办一系列音乐会，但不是通常与图书馆联系在一起的那种音乐：原声乐器、关于鸭子和兔子的歌词、唱歌的木偶。

00:09:20.460 --> 00:09:27.150
<v Alex Blumberg>[MUSIC - "SHOW A SIGN OF LIFE" BY THE HIGH STRUNG]
<v Alex Blumberg>[音乐 - The High Strung乐队的《Show a Sign of Life》]

00:09:27.150 --> 00:09:53.190
<v Ira Glass>No. This would be rock music with electric guitars and huge speakers. This would be, in other words, the state of Michigan's first ever rock and roll library tour. And I know what you're thinking. Libraries: famously quiet. Rock and roll: famously loud. This is the diabolical genius of Bill Harmon's idea: making something appealing by binding it with its exact opposite. Card catalogs packed with Harry Potter and Judy Bloom and this.
<v Ira Glass>不。这将是带有电吉他和巨大扬声器的摇滚乐。换句话说，这将是密歇根州有史以来第一次摇滚图书馆巡演。我知道你在想什么。图书馆：以安静著称。摇滚乐：以吵闹著称。这就是比尔·哈默这个主意的邪恶天才之处：通过将它与它完全相反的东西结合起来，使它变得吸引人。装满哈利·波特、朱迪·布鲁姆和这个的图书卡片目录。

00:09:53.190 --> 00:10:07.310
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - "SHOW A SIGN OF LIFE" BY THE HIGH STRUNG]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - The High Strung乐队的《Show a Sign of Life》]

00:10:07.310 --> 00:10:14.670
<v Ira Glass>The song you're listening to now is from the CD of the band that Bill Harmer booked on his library tour, an indie rock trio out of Detroit called The High Strung.
<v Ira Glass>你现在正在听的这首歌来自比尔·哈默在他的图书馆巡演中预定的乐队CD，这是一家来自底特律的独立摇滚三重奏，名叫The High Strung。

00:10:14.670 --> 00:10:22.040
<v Josh Malerman>We are The High Strung. I'm Josh Malerman. I sing, and play the guitar, and write the songs. Derek?
<v Josh Malerman>我们是The High Strung。我是乔什·马勒曼。我唱歌、弹吉他，并写歌。德里克？

00:10:22.040 --> 00:10:32.030
<v Derek Berk>Oh, I'm Derek Berk. I play the drums, and I fix the van. And I take care of a lot of reality issues.
<v Derek Berk>哦，我是德里克·伯克。我打鼓，我修面包车。我还负责处理很多现实问题。

00:10:32.030 --> 00:10:32.830
<v Alex Blumberg>He's the treasurer.
<v Alex Blumberg>他是财务主管。

00:10:32.830 --> 00:10:33.270
<v Josh Malerman>Yes.
<v Josh Malerman>是的。

00:10:33.270 --> 00:10:37.480
<v Derek Berk>No, I'm the CFO of the band.
<v Derek Berk>不，我是乐队的首席财务官。

00:10:37.480 --> 00:10:45.220
<v Chad Stocker>I'm Chad Stocker, and I play the bass. Most of the time we play at rock clubs.
<v Chad Stocker>我是查德·斯托克，我弹贝斯。我们大部分时间在摇滚俱乐部演出。

00:10:45.220 --> 00:10:50.580
<v Josh Malerman>Yeah, rock clubs. It's like, if you can just picture a smoky bar, a lot of drunk people.
<v Josh Malerman>是的，摇滚俱乐部。就像，如果你能想象一个烟雾缭绕的酒吧，一群喝醉的人。

00:10:50.580 --> 00:10:55.800
<v Derek Berk>With a long-haired sound guy that's probably mad about something.
<v Derek Berk>还有一个长头发的音响师，可能对某事感到愤怒。

00:10:55.800 --> 00:11:09.750
<v Alex Blumberg>The High Strung agreed to the library tour last December, and then they didn't think much about it. But Bill, meanwhile, was going around to library conferences pitching the idea. In the end, he booked 34 library shows, one every other day from June through August.
<v Alex Blumberg>The High Strung在去年12月同意了这次图书馆巡演，然后他们没怎么把这事放在心上。但比尔，同时，一直在图书馆会议上推销这个想法。最后，他预定了34场图书馆演出，从6月到8月，每两天一场。

00:11:09.750 --> 00:11:21.990
<v Alex Blumberg>When he came back to the band with this information a couple of months later, not only had they sort of forgotten about it, but they'd had no idea it was going to consume their entire summer. I talked with them two days before the tour started, and they were a little freaked out, more than a little.
<v Alex Blumberg>当他几个月后带着这个信息回到乐队时，他们不仅把这事忘得差不多了，而且根本不知道这会耗掉他们整个夏天。在巡演开始前两天，我和他们谈过，他们有点被吓坏了，甚至不止一点。

00:11:21.990 --> 00:11:23.400
<v Derek Berk>I'm terrified. You know that?
<v Derek Berk>我吓坏了。你知道吗？

00:11:23.400 --> 00:11:32.070
<v Josh Malerman>No. I know. I know. Absolutely. It's like this weird, unknown thing for a band to do because we're not like a novelty act.
<v Josh Malerman>不。我知道。我完全知道。这对乐队来说是一件奇怪的、未知的事情，因为我们不是那种噱头演出。

00:11:32.070 --> 00:11:38.420
<v Derek Berk>This tour, I think, is geared for sixth-graders to 12th-graders, for the most part, and their parents.
<v Derek Berk>这次巡演，我认为主要针对的是六年级到十二年级的学生，以及他们的父母。

00:11:38.420 --> 00:11:43.340
<v Chad Stocker>I feel like we're almost going to be like tamed beasts. Watch my language.
<v Chad Stocker>我觉得我们几乎要像被驯服的野兽了。注意我的语言。

00:11:43.340 --> 00:11:44.310
<v Josh Malerman>Don't get too close, kids.
<v Josh Malerman>别靠太近了，孩子们。

00:11:44.310 --> 00:11:46.190
<v Chad Stocker>Cover the liquor up off the breath, you know?
<v Chad Stocker>掩盖住呼吸里的酒气，你知道吗？

00:11:46.190 --> 00:11:48.200
<v Josh Malerman>We should be in a cage.
<v Josh Malerman>我们应该被关在笼子里。

00:11:48.200 --> 00:11:57.770
<v Chad Stocker>Yeah. We are playing inside the libraries most the time. And I went to one yesterday, and the guy showing me, he's like, "This is your stage." And it was, like, the reference desk.
<v Chad Stocker>是的。我们大部分时间是在图书馆里演出。我昨天去了其中一个，那个人向我展示，他就像说，“这是你的舞台。”那是，参考资料柜台。

00:11:57.770 --> 00:12:00.040
<v Chad Stocker>[LAUGHTER]
<v Chad Stocker>[笑声]

00:12:00.040 --> 00:12:08.800
<v Chad Stocker>I was like, "Oh. Yeah. Where do we plug in?" He was like, "Plug in?"
<v Chad Stocker>我说，“哦。是的。我们在哪里接通电源？”他说，“接通电源？”

00:12:08.800 --> 00:12:41.240
<v Alex Blumberg>Josh, Derek and Chad have known each other since middle school. They formed their band about five years ago in New York. Early on, Josh booked a couple of live shows so they could get used to playing in front of people. Once they got on the road, they liked it so much they didn't want to quit. They've been touring non-stop now for four years straight. They've played in 42 of the 50 states to all different crowds. In Austin, Texas, they played to an audience of over 1,000. But a show in New Orleans that they drove all night to get to didn't have a single person. Even the bartender left. There was one guy there, but he was passed out drunk the entire show.
<v Alex Blumberg>乔什、德里克和查德从中学起就认识了。他们大约五年前在纽约组建了乐队。早些时候，乔什预定了几场现场演出，这样他们就可以习惯在观众面前表演。一旦他们开始上路，他们就太喜欢这种感觉了，不想停下来。他们现在已经连续四年不停地巡演了。他们在50个州中的42个州为各种不同的观众演出过。在德克萨斯州奥斯汀，他们为超过1000名观众演出。但在新奥尔良的一场演出，他们开车开了一整晚才赶到，却一个人都没有。连调酒师都走了。现场有一个人，但他整场演出都醉得不省人事。

00:12:41.240 --> 00:12:52.990
<v Alex Blumberg>They're in the position of a lot of indie bands: some critical recognition, one CD out, another one due this September, a website, something of a following. In short, a teeny bit of fame and not really any fortune.
<v Alex Blumberg>他们处于许多独立乐队的处境：一些评论界的认可，出了一张CD，另一张定于今年九月，一个网站，还有一些追随者。简而言之，有一点点的名气，但其实没什么财富。

00:12:52.990 --> 00:12:57.060
<v Josh Malerman>We've been living off $10 a day, each, for four years now.
<v Josh Malerman>我们四年来每天靠10美元生活，每个人。

00:12:57.060 --> 00:12:57.550
<v Derek Berk>Right.
<v Derek Berk>对。

00:12:57.550 --> 00:13:04.280
<v Josh Malerman>Which is pretty nuts. No matter how much money we make the night before, we each just get $10 a day.
<v Josh Malerman>这真的很疯狂。无论前一天晚上我们赚多少钱，我们每个人每天只拿10美元。

00:13:04.280 --> 00:13:05.480
<v Alex Blumberg>Who decides that?
<v Alex Blumberg>谁决定的？

00:13:05.480 --> 00:13:06.110
<v Josh Malerman>Our CFO.
<v Josh Malerman>我们的首席财务官。

00:13:06.110 --> 00:13:09.740
<v Derek Berk>Me. I decided that three years ago.
<v Derek Berk>我。我三年前决定的。

00:13:09.740 --> 00:13:10.430
<v Josh Malerman>A brilliant CFO.
<v Josh Malerman>一位出色的首席财务官。

00:13:10.430 --> 00:13:13.310
<v Derek Berk>There's been no adjustment for cost of living.
<v Derek Berk>生活成本完全没有调整。

00:13:13.310 --> 00:13:15.690
<v Derek Berk>[LAUGHTER]
<v Derek Berk>[笑声]

00:13:15.690 --> 00:13:17.170
<v Josh Malerman>He's not very progressive.
<v Josh Malerman>他不怎么开明。

00:13:17.170 --> 00:13:29.550
<v Derek Berk>No. I mean the dollar menu is still the dollar. Sometimes there's a buy-out, which is exciting.
<v Derek Berk>不。我是说美元菜单还是那个价。有时候有“买断费”，这很令人兴奋。

00:13:29.550 --> 00:13:30.130
<v Alex Blumberg>And that's, when?
<v Alex Blumberg>那是什么时候？

00:13:30.130 --> 00:13:44.650
<v Derek Berk>It's when they give you money. Some places you play at their restaurant too. But, if they don't have food there, they'll either order you a pizza, or something, or a burrito, or [UNINTELLIGIBLE]. Or there's the elusive buy-out where they actually give you cash.
<v Derek Berk>就是他们给你钱的时候。有些地方你们也在他们的餐厅演出。但是，如果他们那里没有食物，他们要么会给你点披萨，或者什么，或者墨西哥卷饼，或者[听不清]。或者有那难以捉摸的“买断费”，他们真的给你现金。

00:13:44.650 --> 00:13:45.850
<v Josh Malerman>That's the best.
<v Josh Malerman>那是最好的。

00:13:45.850 --> 00:13:46.830
<v Derek Berk>Then you can buy whatever you want.
<v Derek Berk>那你就可以买任何你想要的东西。

00:13:46.830 --> 00:13:49.820
<v Chad Stocker>Cigarettes and a cheaper sandwich somewhere else.
<v Chad Stocker>香烟和别处更便宜的三明治。

00:13:49.820 --> 00:13:51.220
<v Derek Berk>Right, and pocket the extra money.
<v Derek Berk>对，然后把剩下的钱揣进兜里。

00:13:51.220 --> 00:13:52.530
<v Chad Stocker>Yeah.
<v Chad Stocker>是的。

00:13:52.530 --> 00:13:57.530
<v Alex Blumberg>I thought you meant you would take the money and go get something really, really good. But you're actually [INAUDIBLE] even worse.
<v Alex Blumberg>我以为你的意思是你会拿了钱去买点真的、真的很好的东西。但你其实[听不清]甚至更糟。

00:13:57.530 --> 00:14:07.900
<v Chad Stocker>No. You get something worse than what they would give you so you can save the money.
<v Chad Stocker>不。你会买比他们给你的更差的东西，这样你才能省下钱。

00:14:07.900 --> 00:14:27.380
<v Alex Blumberg>I know this is a digression, but one of the things I found most charming about the band is this fact. A while ago, they saw some footage on TV of an old Motown act. Everyone in the group was wearing the same outfit. And they thought, now, those guys look together up there. We should do that. So, a couple of months ago, they started performing every show in identical uniforms.
<v Alex Blumberg>我知道这跑题了，但乐队最让我着迷的一点是这个事实。不久前，他们在电视上看到了一些老摩城乐队的录像。小组里的每个人都穿着同样的衣服。他们想，看，那些家伙在台上看起来真整齐。我们应该那样做。所以，几个月前，他们开始穿着完全相同的制服参加每一场演出。

00:14:27.380 --> 00:14:46.490
<v Josh Malerman>Tight, white, really tight, white, outfits with blue stars up and down the sides, you know? If you can imagine Evel Knievel, I guess. And it's more fun for me, man, because I feel like a superhero when we wear those outfits. It separates you from the audience, or something, you know what I mean? It makes you into a television show, or a superhero, or something.
<v Josh Malerman>紧身的、白色的、真的很紧的、白色的服装，两侧有蓝色星星，你知道吗？如果你能想象埃维尔·克尼维尔，我想。这对我来说更有趣，伙计，因为当我们穿上那些衣服时，我觉得自己像个超级英雄。它把你和观众分开了，或者别的什么，你懂我意思吗？它把你变成了一个电视节目，或者一个超级英雄，或者别的什么。

00:14:46.490 --> 00:14:50.470
<v Alex Blumberg>So how does that work if you're wearing the same costume every night?
<v Alex Blumberg>那么如果你每天晚上都穿同样的衣服，那怎么弄？

00:14:50.470 --> 00:14:52.920
<v Josh Malerman>Yeah. That's a great follow-up question.
<v Josh Malerman>是的。那是个很好的后续问题。

00:14:52.920 --> 00:14:55.290
<v Chad Stocker>That is good. That's a very intelligent question.
<v Chad Stocker>那是很好的。那是一个非常聪明的问题。

00:14:55.290 --> 00:15:18.010
<v Josh Malerman>For the first time ever, I'm really persistent about us cleaning something, because I want to make sure that we always have these uniforms to wear. And they can be outrageous if we wear them for three shows in a row without washing them, so we've just got to find a laundromat in every city, you know? I hang out. These guys don't. I go and do that. And I'm alone at these weird laundromats in every town we go to.
<v Josh Malerman>我第一次真的坚持要我们清洗什么东西，因为我想确保我们总是有这些制服可以穿。如果我们连续穿三次演出而不洗，它们可能会变得很离谱，所以我们必须在每个城市找到自助洗衣店，你知道吗？我待在那里。他们不。我去了，在每个我们去的城镇那些古怪的自助洗衣店里做这件事。

00:15:18.010 --> 00:15:19.710
<v Chad Stocker>Josh is kind of--
<v Chad Stocker>乔什有点……

00:15:19.710 --> 00:15:20.570
<v Josh Malerman>In charge?
<v Josh Malerman>负责？

00:15:20.570 --> 00:15:23.680
<v Chad Stocker>Josh is kind of in charge of cleaning the laundry.
<v Chad Stocker>乔什有点负责洗衣服。

00:15:23.680 --> 00:15:26.350
<v Chad Stocker>[LAUGHTER]
<v Chad Stocker>[笑声]

00:15:26.350 --> 00:15:38.840
<v Chad Stocker>Cleaning the get-ups. We call them get-ups. We're like, you got the get-ups? Yeah, they're in the bag. There's a get-up bag, too.
<v Chad Stocker>洗那些行头。我们叫它们行头。我们就像，你带了行头吗？是的，在包里。还有一个行头包。

00:15:38.840 --> 00:16:01.790
<v Alex Blumberg>The High Strung, in many ways, are the perfect band for this tour, because they're both very rock and roll and very library. They've all been to college. Chad wears round glasses. Josh says one of his main song writing inspirations is William Faulkner. He actually wrote an outline for his last album in which he describes his 12 songs, each a different take on optimism. And they're huge library patrons, and not just because they like to read.
<v Alex Blumberg>The High Strung在很多方面是这次巡演的完美乐队，因为他们既非常摇滚又非常图书馆风。他们都上过大学。查德戴着圆眼镜。乔什说他主要的歌曲创作灵感之一是威廉·福克纳。他实际上为他的上一张专辑写了一个大纲，在其中他描述了他的12首歌，每一首都是对乐观主义的不同解读。他们是忠实的图书馆常客，不仅仅是因为他们喜欢阅读。

00:16:01.790 --> 00:16:14.510
<v Chad Stocker>The truth is, bands, nowadays, are in libraries all the time because there's the internet and stuff there. And when you're on the road, it's a valuable place to go for a band.
<v Chad Stocker>事实是，乐队现在一直在图书馆，因为那里有互联网之类的东西。当你在路上时，这对乐队来说是一个很有价值的地方。

00:16:14.510 --> 00:16:16.610
<v Josh Malerman>Yeah, It's our office.
<v Josh Malerman>是的，这是我们的办公室。

00:16:16.610 --> 00:16:18.070
<v Chad Stocker>It's like the field office.
<v Chad Stocker>这就像是实地办事处。

00:16:18.070 --> 00:16:23.670
<v Derek Berk>You got internet, and you got a clean toilet with, usually, there's toilet paper and a door that locks.
<v Derek Berk>你有互联网，还有一个干净的厕所，通常有卫生纸，还有一扇能锁上的门。

00:16:23.670 --> 00:16:27.370
<v Alex Blumberg>Wait. Are are you telling me that you guys stop at the library in every--
<v Alex Blumberg>等等。你是说你们在每一个……都要在图书馆停下来吗？

00:16:27.370 --> 00:16:28.570
<v Derek Berk>Yeah. Shave there.
<v Derek Berk>是的。在那里刮胡子。

00:16:28.570 --> 00:16:35.160
<v Josh Malerman>We do. We usually shave there. Yeah. We're in a lot of libraries across the country.
<v Josh Malerman>我们会的。我们通常在那里刮胡子。是的。我们在全国各地的很多图书馆里。

00:16:35.160 --> 00:16:39.880
<v Chad Stocker>I used to work at the Ann Arbor district library as a security guard there in college.
<v Chad Stocker>我上大学时曾在安娜堡区图书馆当过保安。

00:16:39.880 --> 00:16:42.960
<v Josh Malerman>Oh, my gosh. It was all leading to this.
<v Josh Malerman>哦，天哪。一切都是注定的。

00:16:42.960 --> 00:16:56.390
<v Chad Stocker>And we used to have to kick out people that had their shoes off, or if they smelled too bad and got complaints, or if we caught them cleaning up in the bathroom, and stuff. And then, years later, I'm doing that.
<v Chad Stocker>我们以前不得不把那些脱掉鞋子的人赶走，或者如果他们闻起来太臭被投诉了，或者如果我们抓到他们在洗手间洗漱之类的。然后，几年后，我在做那件事。

00:16:56.390 --> 00:17:10.040
<v Alex Blumberg>I don't know if you've risen or fallen.
<v Alex Blumberg>我不知道你是升华了还是堕落了。

00:17:10.040 --> 00:17:42.600
<v Ira Glass>The next time I catch up with the band is at the Escanaba Public Library. It's the band's 25th library in their 34-library tour. And the audiences, they say, have been all over the place. Some have been high school kids, mainly 16- to 18-year-olds. Others, like this crowd, have skewed much younger. It's about 40 kids, mostly between the ages of nine and 14, many of them swinging their legs on those stackable metal library chairs, accompanied by grandparents or parents. The emcee of today's show is the Assistant Children's Librarian, Charlotte Oshe, who does her own librarian version of, hello Escanaba, are you ready to rock?
<v Ira Glass>我下一次赶上乐队是在埃斯卡纳巴公共图书馆。这是乐队34场图书馆巡演中的第25场。他们说，观众的情况各不相同。有些是高中生，主要是16到18岁的孩子。另一些，就像这群人，年龄要小得多。大约有40个孩子，大多在9到14岁之间，许多人摇晃着腿坐在那些可堆叠的金属图书馆椅子上，由祖父母或父母陪同。今天演出的司仪是助理儿童图书馆员夏洛特·奥谢，她用她自己的图书馆员版本说道：“你好埃斯卡纳巴，准备好摇滚了吗？”

00:17:42.600 --> 00:17:51.380
<v Charlotte Oshe>Hello, kids. Congratulations for finishing your summer reading program contracts. We are so proud of you. You are here to see a, what?
<v Charlotte Oshe>你好，孩子们。祝贺你们完成了暑期阅读计划合同。我们为你们感到骄傲。你们来这里是为了看一个，什么？

00:17:51.380 --> 00:17:52.850
<v Girl>Concert?
<v Girl>音乐会？

00:17:52.850 --> 00:17:54.600
<v Charlotte Oshe>Concert. Right.
<v Charlotte Oshe>音乐会。对。

00:17:54.600 --> 00:18:07.610
<v Alex Blumberg>We're in a gray, carpeted conference room off the main floor of the library: fluorescent lights, a white board with a graph showing recent expenditures. This room is normally used for county budget meetings. The band, in regular street clothes for this tour, takes the floor.
<v Alex Blumberg>我们在主楼层旁边一个灰色的地毯铺地的会议室里：荧光灯、一个展示近期支出图表的白板。这个房间通常用于县预算会议。乐队，这次巡演穿着平时的街头服装，占据了地板。

00:18:07.610 --> 00:18:13.620
<v Josh Malerman>How cool is it that your library does this, huh? This is awesome. OK. We are The High Strung. Yeah. Clap for your library. This is great.
<v Josh Malerman>你们图书馆这样做有多酷，是吧？这太棒了。好的。我们是The High Strung。是的。为你们的图书馆鼓掌。这太棒了。

00:18:13.620 --> 00:18:17.600
<v Josh Malerman>[APPLAUSE]
<v Josh Malerman>[掌声]

00:18:17.600 --> 00:18:20.750
<v Josh Malerman>Here It Comes Again . 1, 2, 3, 4.
<v Josh Malerman>《Here It Comes Again》。1，2，3，4。

00:18:20.750 --> 00:18:28.630
<v Josh Malerman>[MUSIC - "HERE IT COMES AGAIN" BY THE HIGH STRUNG]
<v Josh Malerman>[音乐 - The High Strung乐队的《Here It Comes Again》]

00:18:28.630 --> 00:18:47.690
<v Alex Blumberg>The loudness definitely catches people off guard. Kids flinch, startled. Some of them cover their ears. Watching it all, I feel very nervous for the band, for the kids, for the parents who brought the kids, for everyone. When I ask the band about it later, they tell me nobody involved has any idea what to expect.
<v Alex Blumberg>巨大的声音确实让人措手不及。孩子们畏缩了，吓了一跳。有些人捂住耳朵。看着这一切，我为乐队、为孩子们、为带孩子来的家长、为每个人感到非常紧张。当我后来问乐队这件事时，他们告诉我，所有相关的人都不知道会发生什么。

00:18:47.690 --> 00:19:01.840
<v Josh Malerman>It's like, what is going on here? You feel like you're in a sociology experiment. This is let's put the loudest thing known to man inside the conference room of the library with a bunch of 10-year-old kids and see what happens.
<v Josh Malerman>就像，这里发生了什么？你感觉自己置身于一个社会学实验中。这就是把已知最吵的东西放进图书馆的会议室里，和一群10岁的孩子一起，看看会发生什么。

00:19:01.840 --> 00:19:03.230
<v Josh Malerman>[LAUGHS]
<v Josh Malerman>[笑声]

00:19:03.230 --> 00:19:07.600
<v Chad Stocker>There was kids that were scared when we were in Brighton.
<v Chad Stocker>我们在布莱顿的时候有孩子被吓到了。

00:19:07.600 --> 00:19:08.390
<v Alex Blumberg>In Brighton?
<v Alex Blumberg>在布莱顿？

00:19:08.390 --> 00:19:19.920
<v Chad Stocker>Yeah. Even though we were outside, they still had faces of disbelief. Or they were like, this is not what-- everyone's always telling me to be quiet here. This is not supposed to be happening.
<v Chad Stocker>是的。即使我们在外面，他们脸上仍然带着难以置信的神情。或者他们就像是，这不该是……每个人总是告诉我要在这里安静。这不应该发生。

00:19:19.920 --> 00:19:21.110
<v Josh Malerman>They said that to us, right?
<v Josh Malerman>他们对我们说了，对吧？

00:19:21.110 --> 00:19:24.340
<v Chad Stocker>Yeah. Yeah, almost to where they were angered.
<v Chad Stocker>是的。是的，几乎到了他们感到愤怒的地步。

00:19:24.340 --> 00:19:26.190
<v Alex Blumberg>That one girl said that there.
<v Alex Blumberg>那个女孩在那里说了。

00:19:26.190 --> 00:19:30.500
<v Chad Stocker>They were angered. Like, everything everyone's been telling me up to this point has been a lie.
<v Chad Stocker>他们被激怒了。就像，每个人到现在为止告诉我的所有事情都是谎言。

00:19:30.500 --> 00:19:32.900
<v Chad Stocker>[LAUGHS]
<v Chad Stocker>[笑声]

00:19:32.900 --> 00:19:46.670
<v Chad Stocker>[MUSIC - "HERE IT COMES AGAIN" BY HIGH STRUNG]
<v Chad Stocker>[音乐 - The High Strung乐队的《Here It Comes Again》]

00:19:46.670 --> 00:19:59.530
<v Alex Blumberg>In the audience, no one dances or even moves, really. They all sit and watch, attentively but a little passively, like the way you'd watch someone reading aloud during story hour. Between songs, the band throws in some public service-y library messages.
<v Alex Blumberg>在观众席中，没有人跳舞，甚至没有移动，真的。他们都坐着观看，专注但有点被动，就像你在故事时间看别人大声朗读的样子。歌曲之间，乐队会插入一些公共服务式的图书馆信息。

00:19:59.530 --> 00:20:13.230
<v Chad Stocker>Another exciting thing about libraries are-- and this is something that we need, terribly-- are maps and atlases. This is all reference material. You can check it out, but you can't check it out. You know what I'm saying?
<v Chad Stocker>关于图书馆另一件令人兴奋的事是——这是我们非常需要的——是地图和地图集。这些都是参考资料。你可以借阅它们，但你不能把它们带走。你懂我意思吗？

00:20:13.230 --> 00:20:39.140
<v Chad Stocker>So in these maps, we find our way all over the country. And you can even look up crazy places all over the world. I want to know if anyone has been to Sweden or Venezuela? No. OK. The reason I asked because this next song we made a video for for MTV, and if you lived in Sweden or Venezuela, you may have seen it, because that's the only place [INAUDIBLE].
<v Chad Stocker>所以这些地图里，我们找到了遍布全国的路。你甚至可以查阅世界各地的疯狂地方。我想知道有没有人去过瑞典或委内瑞拉？没有。好的。我问的原因是我们为这下一首歌在MTV上拍了录影带，如果你住在瑞典或委内瑞拉，你可能看过它，因为那是唯一的地方[听不清]。

00:20:39.140 --> 00:20:43.600
<v Chad Stocker>This song is called Wretched Boy. 1, 2, 3, 4.
<v Chad Stocker>这首歌叫《Wretched Boy》。1，2，3，4。

00:20:43.600 --> 00:20:54.020
<v Chad Stocker>[MUSIC - "WRETCHED BOY" BY THE HIGH STRUNG]
<v Chad Stocker>[音乐 - The High Strung乐队的《Wretched Boy》]

00:20:54.020 --> 00:21:12.450
<v Alex Blumberg>So here it is, the image of rock and roll and the image of the library going head to head. Bill Harmer hopes that the image of rock and roll will win out and that the library will seem cooler as a result of this bizarre collision. But what if the opposite proves true? The library wins, and this tour does nothing except make the band seem lame?
<v Alex Blumberg>所以，这就是摇滚乐的形象和图书馆的形象正面碰撞。比尔·哈默希望摇滚乐的形象能够胜出，从而使图书馆看起来更酷。但如果相反的结果被证明是真的呢？图书馆赢了，而这次巡演除了让乐队看起来很逊之外什么都没做？

00:21:12.450 --> 00:21:28.830
<v Alex Blumberg>Basically, you are being sort of retained to make this institution that is not necessarily seen as cool cooler. That's sort of a big responsibility. Do you guys feel cool enough?
<v Alex Blumberg>基本上，你有点被聘用来让这个不一定被认为是酷的机构变得更酷。这算是一个很大的责任。你们觉得够酷吗？

00:21:28.830 --> 00:21:31.540
<v Derek Berk>That's a weird thing. That's a really weird thing.
<v Derek Berk>那是件奇怪的事。那真的是件奇怪的事。

00:21:31.540 --> 00:21:32.620
<v Josh Malerman>That's the weirdest question I've ever been asked.
<v Josh Malerman>那是我被问过的最奇怪的问题。

00:21:32.620 --> 00:21:38.400
<v Derek Berk>I think so. I think the public library-- That is a really weird question. I think the public library is really cool.
<v Derek Berk>我想是的。我想公共图书馆——那真是一个非常奇怪的问题。我觉得公共图书馆真的很酷。

00:21:38.400 --> 00:21:39.610
<v Josh Malerman>Sure, yes.
<v Josh Malerman>当然，是的。

00:21:39.610 --> 00:21:45.390
<v Derek Berk>But I think that maybe people don't know it, and maybe teens don't know it.
<v Derek Berk>但我认为也许人们不知道，也许青少年不知道。

00:21:45.390 --> 00:21:54.800
<v Josh Malerman>Are we cooler than the library? No. Are we cooler than the idea of the library? Maybe.
<v Josh Malerman>我们比图书馆更酷吗？不。我们比图书馆这个概念更酷吗？也许。

00:21:54.800 --> 00:22:04.820
<v Alex Blumberg>As the show progresses, it seems less and less like a rock show in a library, and more and more like just a rock show. Josh gets on top of a table for his guitar solo and jumps off at the end.
<v Alex Blumberg>随着演出进行，它看起来越来越不像在图书馆里的摇滚演出，而越来越像是一场单纯的摇滚演出。乔什为了吉他独奏爬上了一张桌子，并在最后跳了下来。

00:22:04.820 --> 00:22:23.030
<v Alex Blumberg>A group of adolescent girls sits in front of Chad, the bass player. He's one of those happy musicians who can't help but smile an adorable smile while he plays super complicated riffs. You can practically see the communal crush develop. Between songs, they flirt with him. One girl complains that her tan this summer came out usually orange and that she looks like an Oompa Loompa.
<v Alex Blumberg>一群青春期的女孩坐在贝斯手查德面前。他是那种快乐的音乐家，在弹奏超级复杂的乐句时忍不住露出可爱的微笑。你几乎可以看到这种共有的迷恋正在滋生。歌曲之间，她们和他调情。一个女孩抱怨她今年夏天的晒黑肤色变成了通常的橙色，看起来像个奥姆帕·伦帕人。

00:22:23.030 --> 00:22:23.520
<v Alex Blumberg>[YOUNG GIRLS LAUGHTER]
<v Alex Blumberg>[年轻女孩的笑声]

00:22:23.520 --> 00:22:24.520
<v Chad Stocker>What?
<v Chad Stocker>什么？

00:22:24.520 --> 00:22:26.010
<v Chad Stocker>[YOUNG GIRLS TALKING ALL AT ONCE]
<v Chad Stocker>[年轻女孩同时说话]

00:22:26.010 --> 00:22:28.010
<v Chad Stocker>Aren't Oompa Loompas green?
<v Chad Stocker>奥姆帕·伦帕人不应该是绿色的吗？

00:22:28.010 --> 00:22:29.500
<v Chad Stocker>[INTERPOSING VOICES]
<v Chad Stocker>[交叉的声音]

00:22:29.500 --> 00:22:37.030
<v Chad Stocker>Oh. I like their pants. Oompa Loompa's got cool, cool clothes. 1, 2, 3.
<v Chad Stocker>哦。我喜欢他们的裤子。奥姆帕·伦帕人穿着酷酷的衣服。1，2，3。

00:22:37.030 --> 00:22:43.420
<v Chad Stocker>[MUSIC PLAYING]
<v Chad Stocker>[播放音乐]

00:22:43.420 --> 00:23:01.550
<v Alex Blumberg>I know the show is going well when, at one point, I notice that Josh has actually broken one of his guitar strings. By the time the head children's librarian comes out and does the librarian version of let's give it up for the band, I said let's give it up for the band, one thing's for sure: this county library conference room has been rocked harder than it's ever been rocked before.
<v Alex Blumberg>我知道演出进行得很顺利，当在某个时刻，我注意到乔什实际上断了一根吉他弦。当儿童图书馆总管理员出来做图书馆员版本的“让我们为乐队鼓掌，我说让我们为乐队鼓掌”时，有一点是肯定的：这个县图书馆会议室被摇滚得比以往任何时候都要强烈。

00:23:01.550 --> 00:23:09.020
<v Charlotte Oshe>Boys and girls, would you truly let these gentlemen know how much we appreciate them? Come on, really let them know.
<v Charlotte Oshe>男孩和女孩们，你们能真正让这些绅士们知道我们有多感激他们吗？来吧，真正让他们知道。

00:23:09.020 --> 00:23:13.280
<v Charlotte Oshe>[APPLAUSE AND SCREAMS]
<v Charlotte Oshe>[掌声和尖叫声]

00:23:13.280 --> 00:23:38.910
<v Alex Blumberg>But the question is, was it rocked hard enough to actually change the image of the library? I talked to scores of kids at three different library shows in three different towns. Maybe half are what you'd call library kids, part of the teen reading club, or on a first-name basis with the local librarian. The rest just saw ads in the paper, or their parents did. But everyone talked about the library tour the way this group of teenagers in Iron Mountain did. They're all standing outside the show, wearing black, heavy metal T-shirts.
<v Alex Blumberg>但问题是，它真的摇滚得足以改变图书馆的形象吗？我和三个不同城镇的三场图书馆演出的几十个孩子谈过。可能有一半人是你所谓的“图书馆孩子”，是青少年阅读俱乐部的成员，或者和当地图书馆员是熟人。其余的人只是在报纸上看到了广告，或者他们的父母看到了。但每个人都谈论着图书馆巡演，就像这群在铁山的青少年一样。他们都站在演出场地外面，穿着黑色的重金属T恤。

00:23:38.910 --> 00:23:46.160
<v Alex Blumberg>So if you had to describe the way you thought of the library before this concert, what would you have said? What were the adjectives that would come to your mind if you thought about that?
<v Alex Blumberg>所以如果你必须描述一下你在这次音乐会之前对图书馆的看法，你会怎么说？如果想到那个，你会想到什么形容词？

00:23:46.160 --> 00:23:46.920
<v Boy>Dull.
<v Boy>沉闷。

00:23:46.920 --> 00:23:48.140
<v Boy>Quiet.
<v Boy>安静。

00:23:48.140 --> 00:23:48.720
<v Boy>Very quiet.
<v Boy>非常安静。

00:23:48.720 --> 00:23:49.820
<v Boy>Activities.
<v Boy>活动。

00:23:49.820 --> 00:23:52.300
<v Alex Blumberg>And now?
<v Alex Blumberg>那现在呢？

00:23:52.300 --> 00:23:53.010
<v Boy>Amazing.
<v Boy>太棒了。

00:23:53.010 --> 00:23:53.820
<v Alex Blumberg>Come on. Seriously?
<v Alex Blumberg>得了吧。认真地？

00:23:53.820 --> 00:23:54.710
<v Boy>Yes.
<v Boy>是的。

00:23:54.710 --> 00:23:55.610
<v Boy>Yeah.
<v Boy>是的。

00:23:55.610 --> 00:23:58.770
<v Boy>It's better than it was before, anyways. I liked it.
<v Boy>反正比以前好多了。我喜欢。

00:23:58.770 --> 00:24:02.310
<v Boy>Yeah, because before it was just old ladies. Now it's young people.
<v Boy>是的，因为以前只是老太太。现在是年轻人了。

00:24:02.310 --> 00:24:03.470
<v Boy>It was a lot of fun.
<v Boy>这真的很有趣。

00:24:03.470 --> 00:24:08.980
<v Alex Blumberg>And then there was this 11-year-old in Menominee, who had planned on going fishing, but decided to check out the rock show instead.
<v Alex Blumberg>然后就是那个在梅诺米尼的11岁孩子，他原计划去钓鱼，但决定改来看摇滚演出。

00:24:08.980 --> 00:24:10.940
<v Alex Blumberg>Did it make you think differently about the library?
<v Alex Blumberg>它让你对图书馆有不同的看法了吗？

00:24:10.940 --> 00:24:32.540
<v Boy>Yes, it did. It made me think about, hey, if librarians could do this, make a library not very much a library with making it loud, basically, anyone could do anything.
<v Boy>是的，它确实。它让我思考，嘿，如果图书馆员能做到这一点，通过让它变得吵闹，使图书馆不再那么像一个图书馆，基本上，任何人都可以做任何事。

00:24:32.540 --> 00:25:01.130
<v Alex Blumberg>The High Strung are enjoying the library tour as well. Librarians, as a group, tend to be better at organizing and promoting rock shows than actual rock promoters, as well as much more likely to address you as honey. Also, it's the first tour they've been on where they don't have to drive to another state after the concert is over. They've all been spending a lot of time with their girlfriends. And, even though they're playing a library show almost every day, because they're mostly during the day and because they're mostly within driving distance of Detroit, it's been a very relaxed summer and, by their standards, lucrative.
<v Alex Blumberg>The High Strung也很享受这次图书馆巡演。图书馆员作为一个群体，往往比真正的摇滚经纪人更擅长组织和推广摇滚演出，而且更有可能称呼你为“亲爱的”。此外，这是他们参加的第一次不需要在音乐会结束后开车去另一个州的巡演。他们都有很多时间和女朋友相处。而且，即使他们几乎每天都在演出，因为它们大多在白天，而且大部分都在底特律的开车距离之内，这是一个非常轻松的夏天，按照他们的标准，收益颇丰。

00:25:01.130 --> 00:25:02.750
<v Alex Blumberg>How much are you guys getting paid for this?
<v Alex Blumberg>你们这次拿了多少钱？

00:25:02.750 --> 00:25:07.530
<v Derek Berk>Oh, that's none of your damned business.
<v Derek Berk>哦，那不关你的事。

00:25:07.530 --> 00:25:09.660
<v Josh Malerman>More than we normally get, I would say.
<v Josh Malerman>比我们通常得到的多，我想。

00:25:09.660 --> 00:25:11.450
<v Chad Stocker>On the high end of average.
<v Chad Stocker>平均水平的高端。

00:25:11.450 --> 00:25:14.520
<v Josh Malerman>High end of average for what we usually make at our shows.
<v Josh Malerman>是我们演出通常赚的钱的平均水平的高端。

00:25:14.520 --> 00:25:14.900
<v Chad Stocker>Yeah.
<v Chad Stocker>是的。

00:25:14.900 --> 00:25:27.120
<v Josh Malerman>And we won't have to wait at the end of the night for some promoter to sell a bunch of cocaine down the street to have enough money to pay for our guarantee. You know?
<v Josh Malerman>而且我们不需要在深夜等什么经纪人去街上卖一堆可卡因才有足够的钱来支付我们的保障金。你知道吗？

00:25:27.120 --> 00:25:38.420
<v Alex Blumberg>Overall, the band is surprised how similar the library tour has been to a regular tour, except for one thing. On a regular tour, they don't stick around for a Q&A 20 minutes after every show.
<v Alex Blumberg>总的来说，乐队很惊讶图书馆巡演和普通巡演有多相似，除了有一件事。在普通巡演中，他们不会在每场演出后留下来进行20分钟的问答。

00:25:38.420 --> 00:25:44.880
<v Girl>Some people in this room would like to know if you have a girlfriend, and how old you are.
<v Girl>这个房间里有些人想知道你们是否有女朋友，以及你们多大了。

00:25:44.880 --> 00:25:49.510
<v Boy>How did your families take it when you decided to start a rock band?
<v Boy>当你们决定组建摇滚乐队时，你们的家人有什么反应？

00:25:49.510 --> 00:25:51.490
<v Girl>Do you guys live together?
<v Girl>你们住在一起吗？

00:25:51.490 --> 00:25:54.670
<v Girl>Did you guys read and go to libraries when you were teens?
<v Girl>你们青少年时阅读并去图书馆吗？

00:25:54.670 --> 00:26:03.810
<v Girl>Why isn't the drummer wearing any shoe laces?
<v Girl>为什么鼓手没有系鞋带？

00:26:03.810 --> 00:26:32.460
<v Josh Malerman>I didn't think of this before we started this whole thing, but I really think that, a lot of kids, this is their first rock show, and I did not think of that angle at all when this whole thing started. Like, wow, we really are-- I really remember the first time I saw a bunch of grown men dancing around, making noise together, and people watching. It's like that's an experience. So yeah. And surprised I just never thought of that.
<v Josh Malerman>我们在开始这一切之前没有想到这一点，但我真的认为，对许多孩子来说，这是他们的第一场摇滚演出，我在这一切开始时根本没有想到那个角度。就像，哇，我们真的是——我真的记得我第一次看到一群成年男人在一起跳舞、制造噪音，而人们在观看。这就像那是一种经历。所以是的。我很惊讶我竟然从来没想过。

00:26:32.460 --> 00:27:05.950
<v Josh Malerman>And now, I can't really explain it. The delivery of it all just has a different meaning to me now. It's more weighted or something. Like, oh, wow, this move here, or when I turn to Derek and both of us just do the silliest thing like slam our instruments at the same time, that's going to be an image in that child's mind. They're going to remember that when they remember their first concert, or whatever. So suddenly, things are not weighted with pressure, but weighted with a little more meaning.
<v Josh Malerman>而现在，我真的无法解释。所有这些的传递对我来说现在有了不同的意义。它更沉重或别的什么。就像，哦，哇，这个动作，或者当我转向德里克，我们俩只是做一些最愚蠢的事情，比如同时重击我们的乐器，那将是那个孩子脑海中的一个形象。当他们想起他们的第一场音乐会或什么的，他们会记得那个。所以突然间，事情不再充满压力，而是带有了一点更多的意义。

00:27:05.950 --> 00:27:26.580
<v Josh Malerman>[MUSIC - "CLOWN CAR" BY THE HIGH STRUNG]
<v Josh Malerman>[音乐 - The High Strung乐队的《Clown Car》]

00:27:26.580 --> 00:27:42.890
<v Alex Blumberg>The last show I attend is in Menominee, a small town in the far north of the state. The band is playing in the library central reading room. You couldn't make it up any better. It's a beautiful place with big picture windows looking out on the water and a skylight, rows of bookshelves spread out to either side.
<v Alex Blumberg>我参加的最后一场演出是在梅诺米尼，一个位于该州远北的小镇。乐队在图书馆中央阅览室演出。你编不出比这更好的场景了。那是一个美丽的地方，有看向水面的大落地窗和天窗，一排排书架向两边延伸。

00:27:42.890 --> 00:28:29.120
<v Alex Blumberg>The crowd is the biggest age range I've seen. There's a couple of high school kids with the neo-ska punk look, several middle-schoolers. In the very front row, a kid who can't be more than four years old holding a huge deck of Pokemon cards in one hand and a stuffed bunny in the other. He's covering his ears with his elbows, but he's bouncing wildly up and down on his chair, and he's got a huge smile on his face. A lot of the kids in the audience have a look like that, like I can't explain what I'm watching, but I really like it. When I talk to them about it later, it's hard for them to articulate. "I like the way it made me feel," they say. Or "I like the way one of them will be going one way and one another." Or simply, "I like the way it vibrated on me. That was the funnest part."
<v Alex Blumberg>人群是我见过的年龄跨度最大的。有几个带有新斯卡朋克风格的高中生，几个中学生。在最前排，一个不超过四岁的孩子，一只手拿着一大叠宝可梦卡，另一只手拿着一只毛绒兔子。他用手肘捂着耳朵，但在椅子上疯狂地弹跳，脸上带着巨大的笑容。观众中的许多孩子都有那样的表情，就像我无法解释我正在看什么，但我真的很喜欢它。当我后来和他们谈起这件事时，他们很难表达清楚。“我喜欢它让我感觉到的方式，”他们说。“或者我喜欢他们其中一个向这边走，另一个向那边走的方式。”或者简单地说，“我喜欢它震动我的方式。那是最好玩的部分。”

00:28:29.120 --> 00:28:44.640
<v Alex Blumberg>Of course, if you're selling teens on the library by saying now it's a place that rocks, then you pretty much have to keep booking rock shows. And Bill Harmer would be fine with that. He'd love it if rock and roll were as common in Michigan's libraries as story corner. His colleagues may take some convincing, though.
<v Alex Blumberg>当然，如果你通过说“现在这里是一个摇滚的地方”来向青少年推销图书馆，那么你基本上必须继续预定摇滚演出。比尔·哈默对那样做没问题。如果摇滚乐在密歇根州的图书馆里像故事角一样普遍，他会很高兴的。尽管他的同事们可能需要一些说服。

00:28:44.640 --> 00:29:05.120
<v Alex Blumberg>During the show in Menominee, I went downstairs to the check-out desk. All the librarians had their hands over their ears, or shouting at each other to be heard. I asked them how many of these concerts could they stand each year. One of the youth services librarians, a balding man with a mustache, didn't even try to talk over the noise. He just held up his hand with his index finger to his thumb. Zero, he mouthed.
<v Alex Blumberg>在梅诺米尼的演出期间，我下楼去了借阅台。所有的图书馆员都捂着耳朵，或者互相大喊大叫以便被听到。我问他们每年能忍受多少场这样的音乐会。其中一位青少年服务图书馆员，一个留着胡子谢顶的男人，甚至没有试图盖过噪音。他只是举起手，用食指对准大拇指。零，他对着嘴型说。

00:29:05.120 --> 00:29:10.540
