<v Alex Blumberg>[掌声]

00:29:11.010 --> 00:29:27.210
<v Ira Glass>Alex Blumberg is one of the producers of our program. The High Strung are on the internet at www.thehighstrung.com. It's been a year since Alex did that story. And this summer, The High Strung have expanded to a nationwide library tour: 60 libraries, 42 states.
<v Ira Glass>亚历克斯·布伦伯格是我们节目的制片人之一。The High Strung在互联网上的网址是www.thehighstrung.com。亚历克斯做那个报道已经一年了。而今年夏天，The High Strung已经扩展到全国图书馆巡演：60个图书馆，42个州。

00:29:27.210 --> 00:29:56.940
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - "SHUT UP IN THE LIBRARY" BY BARRY LOUIS POLISAR]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - Barry Louis Polisar的《Shut Up in the Library》]

00:29:56.940 --> 00:31:10.040
<v Ira Glass>Coming up, Jonathan Goldstein and his own father. Quien es mas macho? That's in a minute, from Chicago Public Radio and Public Radio International, when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>接下来，乔纳森·戈德斯坦和他的父亲。谁更阳刚？那在一分钟后，来自芝加哥公共广播电台和公共广播国际公司，当我们的节目继续时。

00:31:10.040 --> 00:31:35.170
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life . I'm Ira Glass. Each week on our program, of course, we choose some theme, bring you a variety of different kinds of stories on that theme. Today's program, Image Makers, stories of people and institutions who are worried what the world thinks of them, and who take action, decisive action. In this half of the show, we have a son who wants to know what his father makes of him, and a father who needs a little image boost with his own son. Let's move on to Act Two of our program.
<v Ira Glass>这是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。每周在我们的节目中，当然，我们都会选择一个主题，为您带来关于该主题的各种不同类型的故事。今天的节目，“形象制造者”，关于那些担心世界怎么看他们，并采取行动、果断行动的人和机构的故事。在节目的这一半，我们有一位想知道他父亲如何看待他的儿子，还有一位在他儿子面前需要一点形象提升的父亲。让我们进入我们节目的第二幕。

00:31:35.170 --> 00:31:49.980
<v Ira Glass>Act Two, Goldstein On Goldstein. This second story's a subject that perhaps we don't get enough of here on the public radio: manliness. I find that even the men I know who you think would be least concerned with manliness are still completely concerned with it.
<v Ira Glass>第二幕，“戈德斯坦论戈德斯坦”。这第二个故事是一个我们在这里的公共广播中可能谈论不够的主题：男子气概。我发现即使是我认识的那些你认为最不关心男子气概的男人，他们仍然完全关心它。

00:31:49.980 --> 00:32:15.380
<v Ira Glass>I have this friend, Michael, who is constantly talking about what is manly and what is not manly. If you do something that he thinks is impressive, the way that he tells you that he thinks it's impressive is he says to you, very manly. He says it all the time. And he's gay. He's a gay. It's almost like being a gay has made him more obsessed with manliness than any of my straight friends. If you're a man, I think it just comes with the territory.
<v Ira Glass>我有这个朋友，迈克尔，他一直在谈论什么是阳刚，什么是不阳刚。如果你做了一件他认为令人印象深刻的事，他告诉你的方式就是说：非常阳刚。他总是这么说。而他是同性恋。他是个男同性恋。这几乎就像做同性恋让他比我任何直男朋友都更痴迷于男子气概。如果你是个男人，我想这只是随之而来的。

00:32:15.380 --> 00:32:19.870
<v Ira Glass>Jonathan Goldstein's been thinking about his own manliness lately. He has this story.
<v Ira Glass>乔纳森·戈德斯坦最近一直在思考他自己的男子气概。他有这个故事。

00:32:19.870 --> 00:32:36.900
<v Jonathan Goldstein>I've always assumed that my father, Buzz Goldstein, has never seen me as an especially manly guy. As a kid, I did not play sports. I had no interest in tools, and I did not dream of muscle cars. My father, though, was an old-school manly man.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>我一直以为我的父亲，巴兹·戈德斯坦，从来没觉得我是个特别阳刚的男人。小时候，我不玩体育运动。我对工具没兴趣，也不梦想开肌肉车。然而，我的父亲是一个老派的硬汉。

00:32:36.900 --> 00:33:24.770
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Growing up, I watched him charm his way out of traffic tickets, whistle for cabs, and say things like va-va-voom, all things I cannot now help but think of as being a part of a manliness from a bygone era. When he poured whiskey, it was always into a washed-out mustard jar, and it was always at least three or four ounces in a shot. Or the way he had of saying, "Listen pal" in his undershirt and flip-flops, his very extended finger, as rigid as a pool cue, aimed straight at your face, never failed to let you know, in no uncertain terms, who was the alpha-est male in the room. Even the random things my father did seemed drenched in a kind of casual, hairy-knuckled machismo. Like, the fact that he liked grape soda made that soda seem infinitely more barrel-chested than a gutless glass of strawberry pop or a [? fay ?] can of nectar.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>成长过程中，我看着他用魅力摆脱交通罚单，为出租车打口哨，并说出“哇哇”之类的词，所有这些我如今不禁认为属于某种过往时代的男子气概。当他倒威士忌时，总是倒进一个洗干净的芥末罐里，一次至少倒三四盎司。或者他穿着汗衫和人字拖说“听着，伙计”的样子，他那根极度伸出的手指，像台球杆一样僵硬，直指你的脸，从没错过让你知道，毫无疑问，谁是房间里最顶级的雄性。甚至我父亲做的随意事情似乎都浸透了一种随意的、长满汗毛的硬汉气概。比如，他喜欢葡萄苏打水的事实，让那种苏打水看起来比一杯胆怯的草莓汽水或一罐花蜜看起来更雄壮。

00:33:24.770 --> 00:33:47.110
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Another thing that I consider manly about my father is that he is a loud man. He is genetically incapable of whispering. When we go to movies, he will turn to me right in the middle and say, just like we were sitting alone, "Isn't that the guy who played the father on The Rockford Files?"
<v Jonathan Goldstein>我认为我父亲男子气概的另一件事是他是一个大嗓门的男人。他基因上无法耳语。当我们去看电影时，他会在中间直接转向我说，就像我们独自坐着一样，“那不是在《洛克福德档案》里扮演父亲的那个家伙吗？”

00:33:47.110 --> 00:34:23.910
<v Jonathan Goldstein>My dad is now 70, and he still keeps curling weights beside his bed. I am 35, and my own brand of manliness, a gentler, less in your face and, dare I say, woosier form of manliness, is something I've always attributed to a generational difference between my father and me. My father grew up in the Bronx in the '40s, a time and place where threatening out your window to strangle someone's barking dog was merely a friendly morning hello. I wanted to talk with my father about the things that he holds to be manly, and maybe in so doing, figure out what it was that I lacked back then, and still lack to this day.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>我爸爸现在70岁了，他床边还放着用来练肱二头肌的哑铃。我35岁，我自己的男子气概——一种更温和、不那么咄咄逼人，甚至可以说更软弱的男子气概——是我一直归因于我和父亲之间代际差异的东西。我父亲在40年代的布朗克斯长大，那个时代和地点，在窗外威胁要勒死某人的吠叫的狗仅仅是一个友好的清晨问候。我想和父亲谈谈他认为阳刚的事情，也许通过这样做，能弄清楚我当时缺少什么，以及至今仍缺少什么。

00:34:23.910 --> 00:34:29.030
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Do you think there's certain things that a man should know how to do, like how to handle himself? Should he know how to box a little bit?
<v Jonathan Goldstein>你认为男人应该知道怎么做某些事吗，比如怎么处理他自己？他应该知道怎么打一点拳击吗？

00:34:29.030 --> 00:34:36.050
<v Buzz Goldstein>Yes. Yeah. A man should know how to defend himself. In other words, a man should not have to take any guff from anybody.
<v Buzz Goldstein>是的。是的。男人应该知道怎么保护自己。换句话说，男人不应该忍受任何人的气。

00:34:36.050 --> 00:34:43.060
<v Jonathan Goldstein>And my father doesn't. Whereas, above my head, there is a sign that reads, please deposit guff.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>而我的父亲不会。然而，在我的头顶上，有一块标牌写着：请存放气。

00:34:43.060 --> 00:35:06.540
<v Jonathan Goldstein>When I was eight, my father decided to teach me how to box. We stood in the foyer of our apartment, knuckle to knuckle, the transgressiveness of it making me giddy and, as my mother would say, overheated. To my eight-year-old brain, the idea of play-fighting with my father ranked somewhere between eating a sidewalk-length roll of candy buttons and watching the Globetrotters on the Wide World of Sports.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>我八岁时，父亲决定教我打拳击。我们站在公寓的门厅里，指关节碰着指关节，这种背德感让我头晕目眩，而且正如我母亲所说，过热。对我八岁的大脑来说，和父亲假打的想法排在吃一卷人行道长的糖果钮扣和看《全球体育大观》里的环球旅行者之间。

00:35:06.540 --> 00:35:28.340
<v Jonathan Goldstein>My father stood beneath the chandelier, dodging, weaving, and jabbing, like a young Willie Pep. I thought this was great. With my arms doing a kind of Dutch windmill, I leapt into the fray, and almost immediately, my mouth connected with one of his fists. My lip split and began to leak blood all over the powder blue, shag carpeting. I started to cry.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>父亲站在吊灯下，躲闪、编织、刺拳，就像年轻时的威利·佩普。我觉得这太棒了。我的手臂像荷兰风车一样摆动，我冲进战斗，几乎立刻，我的嘴撞上了他的拳头之一。我的嘴唇裂开了，开始往粉蓝色的长绒地毯上流血。我开始哭。

00:35:28.340 --> 00:35:53.980
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Then I have this memory of my mother on my father's back, pulling at his hair in a crazed attempt to keep him from mauling me. I cried even harder. I was crying because I had never been hit in the face before, and the pain was so completely new and alien to me. But I was also crying because I felt so bad for my father. Here he was, just trying out his brand of father and son kibitzing with his kid, and now he had to deal with my blood on his hands.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>然后我有这个关于我母亲在我父亲背上的记忆，疯狂地拉扯他的头发，试图阻止他伤害我。我哭得更厉害了。我哭是因为我以前从未被击中过脸，而那种疼痛对我来说是如此完全陌生和异类。但我也哭是因为我为父亲感到难过。他在这里，只是想和他的孩子试着进行他那种父子间的闲聊，而现在他必须处理我手上的血。

00:35:53.980 --> 00:36:20.310
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Even so, a few days later at a family get-together, with my father by my side, I couldn't stop myself from showing every single person I met my puffed-out lip. Then, excitedly, I would tell them that it was my father who had given me this puffed-out lip. Our brawl in the foyer would be the last time my father and I would ever play-fight together.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>即便如此，几天后在一次家庭聚会上，父亲在我身边，我无法克制自己向我遇到的每个人展示我肿胀的嘴唇。然后，兴奋地，我会告诉他们是我父亲给了我这个肿胀的嘴唇。我们在门厅里的那场斗殴将是我父亲和我最后一次一起假打。

00:36:20.310 --> 00:36:24.090
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Do you feel that smoking lends a man a certain kind of manliness?
<v Jonathan Goldstein>你觉得吸烟给男人一种某种程度的男子气概吗？

00:36:24.090 --> 00:36:27.550
<v Buzz Goldstein>Oh, yeah. Don't forget, we grew up with a certain stereotype when we were young.
<v Buzz Goldstein>哦，是的。别忘了，我们年轻时成长在某种刻板印象中。

00:36:27.550 --> 00:36:41.930
<v Jonathan Goldstein>You might not remember this, but I remember one time we were on our way to, maybe it was a bar mitzvah. We stopped at a gas station to get some gas, and you picked me up a pack of cigarettes. And you said that it's important for a young man to smoke at a social gathering.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>你可能不记得这个，但我记得有一次我们在去参加，也许是成年礼的路上。我们在加油站停下来加油，你帮我买了一包烟。你说年轻人参加社交聚会吸烟很重要。

00:36:41.930 --> 00:36:43.770
<v Buzz Goldstein>I did? How old were you at the time?
<v Buzz Goldstein>我做了吗？你那时多大？

00:36:43.770 --> 00:36:46.160
<v Jonathan Goldstein>I was probably about, maybe 19.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>我可能大约，也许19岁。

00:36:46.160 --> 00:37:09.970
<v Buzz Goldstein>Really? I did, eh? Well, because, probably, at that time, that was my way of thinking. Why did I start smoking? Not because I enjoyed smoking. The reason I smoked was because I was, first of all, I was never as tall as my friends. I was always shorter for my age, and it made me feel taller. It made me feel as if I belonged, and it gave me something to do with my hands.
<v Buzz Goldstein>真的吗？我做了，呃？嗯，因为，可能，在那个时候，那是我的思维方式。我为什么开始吸烟？不是因为我喜欢吸烟。我吸烟的原因是因为我，首先，我从来没像我的朋友们那么高。我总是比同龄人矮，这让我觉得更高。它让我觉得我属于那里，并且它给了我一些事来做我的手。

00:37:09.970 --> 00:37:17.540
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Wait. Just go back for a second. How did smoking make you feel taller?
<v Jonathan Goldstein>等等。回去一下。吸烟怎么让你觉得更高？

00:37:17.540 --> 00:37:22.800
<v Buzz Goldstein>Because all the people, at that time, all the people were smoking. My father smoked. My brother was smoking.
<v Buzz Goldstein>因为所有的人，在那个时候，所有的人都在吸烟。我父亲吸烟。我哥哥在吸烟。

00:37:22.800 --> 00:37:24.350
<v Jonathan Goldstein>How old were you when you started smoking?
<v Jonathan Goldstein>你开始吸烟时多大？

00:37:24.350 --> 00:37:27.820
<v Buzz Goldstein>15. So I started at a pretty young age.
<v Buzz Goldstein>15岁。所以我开始得很早。

00:37:27.820 --> 00:37:37.400
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Wait. If I was a taller man, would you have felt that smoking was as important?
<v Jonathan Goldstein>等等。如果我是一个更高的男人，你会觉得吸烟很重要吗？

00:37:37.400 --> 00:37:38.630
<v Buzz Goldstein>Probably, yes.
<v Buzz Goldstein>可能，是的。

00:37:38.630 --> 00:37:39.380
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Yeah?
<v Jonathan Goldstein>是吗？

00:37:39.380 --> 00:37:41.720
<v Buzz Goldstein>Yeah. Yeah.
<v Buzz Goldstein>是的。是的。

00:37:41.720 --> 00:38:07.540
<v Jonathan Goldstein>But still, during my teen years, on the odd occasion when I did smoke, rather than taking my cues from Humphrey Bogart or Danny Zuko, gritting the butt between my teeth like I meant business and ashing often with forceful, deliberate taps, I chose to smoke like David Bowie circa 1972, allowing my cigarette to hang limply from my lips, pretending to be too whacked out on goof balls to care that my ash was six inches long.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>但即便如此，在我十几岁的时候，偶尔我吸烟时，与其模仿亨弗莱·鲍嘉或丹尼·祖科，咬紧牙关像个办正事的人，并且用有力、刻意的敲击频繁掸烟灰，我选择像1972年前后的大卫·鲍伊那样吸烟，让我的香烟松弛地挂在嘴唇上，假装被那些药丸搞得太迷糊，不在乎我的烟灰有六英寸长。

00:38:07.540 --> 00:38:32.570
<v Jonathan Goldstein>My obsession with David Bowie, a languidly, androgynous glam rocker, wasn't something that troubled my father so much as it simply wasn't anything he could understand. My father preferred singers like Joe Cocker, men who deliver their lyrics as though painfully screaming them from a locked toilet stall. In his day, liking someone like David Bowie would have been the domain of degenerate officers in black and white movies about Nazis.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>我对大卫·鲍伊这个懒散的、雌雄同体的华丽摇滚歌手的痴迷，并没有让父亲那么困扰，只是他根本无法理解这件事。父亲更喜欢乔·科克那种歌手，那种交付歌词的方式好像是从锁上的厕所隔间里痛苦地尖叫出来的人。在他那个时代，喜欢大卫·鲍伊这样的人会是关于纳粹的黑白电影里堕落军官的领域。

00:38:32.570 --> 00:38:49.730
<v Jonathan Goldstein>But to this day, whenever I'm over at my parents and Bowie's on Entertainment Tonight, my father will call me over and say, your pal's on TV. And we will both sit there in silence watching David Bowie, both of us wondering what the other one could possibly be thinking.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>但至今，每当我在父母家，电视上在放鲍伊时，父亲就会叫我过去说，你的伙伴在电视上。我们俩就会坐在那里沉默地看着大卫·鲍伊，我们俩都在想对方可能在想什么。

00:38:49.730 --> 00:38:58.410
<v Jonathan Goldstein>The thing is, my father is much more forthright when it comes to letting my friends know what he's thinking. With my friends, he is forthright on a great many subjects.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>问题是，我的父亲在让我的朋友们知道他在想什么时要直率得多。对我的朋友们，他在很多话题上都很直率。

00:38:58.410 --> 00:39:13.120
<v Jonathan Goldstein>If we're downtown, and we're hanging around with one of my friends like, say, Howard, you'll be more inclined to point out a good-looking woman, or something like that, in a way that you probably wouldn't do with me as much.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>如果我们在市区，和我的某个朋友待在一起，比如，霍华德，你更倾向于指出一个好看的女人，或类似的事，方式可能和你对我不太一样。

00:39:13.120 --> 00:39:33.630
<v Buzz Goldstein>Yeah. I guess I'd feel more comfortable because you're my son. You know, these are things that you don't do with your son. But I've pointed out good-looking women with you, Johnny. I've walked with you. I mean, look, I've got eyes in my head. My father did it with me. He'd look at a woman and say, "Oh boy, va-va-voom!"
<v Buzz Goldstein>是的。我猜我会觉得更舒服，因为你是我的儿子。你知道，这些事你不会和你的儿子做。但我确实和你指出过好看的女人，强尼。我和你走在一起。我是说，看，我头上有眼睛。我父亲对我这样做。他会看着一个女人说，“哦，天哪，哇哇！”

00:39:33.630 --> 00:39:56.620
<v Jonathan Goldstein>There was one time I remember, where we were walking through, I think it was a parking lot. And I was, maybe, about 18, 19. And we were passing by this other young woman. And you turned to me. And you noticed that that woman looked at me, and you said, "You see that woman over there? I could tell that you could make her."
<v Jonathan Goldstein>有一次我记得，我们正走过，我想是在一个停车场。我当时，可能，大约18、19岁。我们正经过另一个年轻女性。你转向我。你注意到那个女人看着我，你说，“你看到那个女人了吗？我能看出来你肯定能搞定她。”

00:39:56.620 --> 00:40:00.180
<v Buzz Goldstein>Yeah.
<v Buzz Goldstein>是的。

00:40:00.180 --> 00:40:06.200
<v Jonathan Goldstein>And then you said, "You see, I could tell these things. You can't tell these kind of things because you're more into books."
<v Jonathan Goldstein>然后你说，“你看，我能看出这些事。你看不出这些事，因为你更沉迷于书本。”

00:40:06.200 --> 00:40:13.960
<v Buzz Goldstein>You were more preoccupied, yeah. You were more preoccupied. These weren't the things that were of the greatest interest to you.
<v Buzz Goldstein>你更全神贯注，是的。你更全神贯注。这些不是你最感兴趣的事。

00:40:13.960 --> 00:40:16.360
<v Jonathan Goldstein>And so you thought maybe I would miss something like that?
<v Jonathan Goldstein>所以你以为我可能错过那样的东西？

00:40:16.360 --> 00:40:29.090
<v Buzz Goldstein>Yeah. I guess I was more aware of these things. You know, as a young man, I went out with a lot of women. I had a lot of experience and I could instinctively tell these things.
<v Buzz Goldstein>是的。我猜我更了解这些事。你知道，作为一个年轻人，我和很多女人出去过。我有过很多经验，我可以本能地看出这些事。

00:40:29.090 --> 00:40:33.450
<v Jonathan Goldstein>And so how would you characterize that difference between me and you?
<v Jonathan Goldstein>那么你会怎么描述我和你之间的那个差异？

00:40:33.450 --> 00:40:43.110
<v Buzz Goldstein>I was a different type. I wanted to wear nice clothes, and go out with good-looking women, and drive a nice car. And you were never interested in these sort of things.
<v Buzz Goldstein>我是一个不同类型的人。我想穿漂亮的衣服，和好看的女人出去，开一辆好车。而你从来对这些事没兴趣。

00:40:43.110 --> 00:40:44.150
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Mm-hmm.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>嗯嗯。

00:40:44.150 --> 00:41:14.210
<v Ira Glass>I've always taken my father saying that I was more into books or I was never into these kinds of things as being a polite way of saying that I just wasn't a regular guy. And I have to say, I've never felt hurt by that, nor have I taken it personally. From a young age, I've always believed that defying familial expectations is an important part of self-actualization. I learned that in books.
<v Ira Glass>我一直把我父亲说我更沉迷于书本或从来对这些事没兴趣当作是一种礼貌的说法，意思是说我只是不是个普通的家伙。我必须说，我从来没因此感到受伤，也没往心里去。从小我就一直相信，违抗家庭期望是自我实现的重要组成部分。我在书里学到了这一点。

00:41:14.210 --> 00:41:18.220
<v Buzz Goldstein>Whining is unmanly, complaining and bitching a lot.
<v Buzz Goldstein>抱怨是不阳刚的，抱怨和发牢骚太多。

00:41:18.220 --> 00:41:23.130
<v Jonathan Goldstein>So do you think there's a certain kind of stoicism, that that's necessary?
<v Jonathan Goldstein>所以你认为某种程度的坚忍是必要的吗？

00:41:23.130 --> 00:41:29.090
<v Buzz Goldstein>I don't think. It can't be forced. It's either you have it or you don't.
<v Buzz Goldstein>我不认为。它不能强求。要么你有，要么你没有。

00:41:29.090 --> 00:41:32.740
<v Jonathan Goldstein>When you think of me, do you think of me as possessing a certain quiet manliness?
<v Jonathan Goldstein>当你想到我时，你觉得我拥有某种安静的男子气概吗？

00:41:32.740 --> 00:41:33.520
<v Buzz Goldstein>Yes.
<v Buzz Goldstein>是的。

00:41:33.520 --> 00:41:34.510
<v Jonathan Goldstein>No, I want you to be honest.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>不，我希望你诚实。

00:41:34.510 --> 00:41:52.590
<v Buzz Goldstein>I'm being honest, Johnny, because I feel that you have done a lot of things that I wish I had done when I was younger. You were more assertive with things that you didn't-- you see, I went along with things a lot, whereas you didn't. You see?
<v Buzz Goldstein>我很诚实，强尼，因为我觉得你做了很多事，我希望我年轻时也做了。你对那些事更自信……你看，我经常随波逐流，而你没有。你看？

00:41:52.590 --> 00:42:04.320
<v Buzz Goldstein>Let me give you an example of what I mean. You know my mother was a very domineering woman, even after I married. I just accepted the decisions made by them, and that was unmanly, yes.
<v Buzz Goldstein>让我给你举个例子。你知道我母亲是个非常专横的女人，即使在我结婚后。我只是接受了他们做的决定，那是不阳刚的，是的。

00:42:04.320 --> 00:42:12.570
<v Jonathan Goldstein>My father's mother had talked my father into quitting high school so he could get a job and help support the family. It was a move he always regretted.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>我父亲的母亲说服父亲退学，这样他就可以找份工作帮助支持家庭。那是一个他一直后悔的举动。

00:42:12.570 --> 00:42:33.520
<v Jonathan Goldstein>I remember my father's mother as a small woman with a knack for getting my father to do whatever she wanted. Like, if she needed a lift to the hairdresser in the middle of the day, he would drop everything at once and go. The phone would ring. He would listen in silence, and he would go. Even once my father was married, his mother would still interfere in all sorts of small day-to-day things.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>我记得我父亲的母亲是一个小个子女人，有一手让父亲做任何她想做的事的本领。比如，如果她需要在中午搭便车去理发师那儿，他会放下一切立刻去。电话会响。他会沉默地听着，然后他就去了。即使我父亲结婚后，他的母亲仍然会干涉各种各样的小事。

00:42:33.520 --> 00:43:08.920
<v Buzz Goldstein>For example, we were shopping one day on Kings Highway, your mother and I, and we saw a cute fireplace. It wasn't a real fireplace, it was an electric fireplace. And we fell in love with it. It was black metal, and we were ready to buy it. And I happened to bring up the topic to my mother and she just pooh-poohed, and just discouraged us, and discouraged me. And then I changed my mind as a result of that. It was impractical. And what do you need this for? And what are you wasting your money on garbage like that?
<v Buzz Goldstein>比如，有一天我们在国王高速公路上购物，你母亲和我，我们看到了一个可爱的壁炉。那不是一个真的壁炉，那是一个电壁炉。我们爱上了它。它是黑色的金属，我们准备买下它。我碰巧把话题提给了我母亲，她直接否定了，只是劝阻我们，劝阻我。然后我因为那个改变了主意。它不切实际。你需要这个干什么？你把钱浪费在那种垃圾上干什么？

00:43:08.920 --> 00:43:43.230
<v Buzz Goldstein>And I'll be honest with you, that was unmanly. OK, if you want to talk about being unmanly, that was unmanly. I should have taken a strong position in saying, look, this is between me and my wife. We enjoy this. It's going to give us happiness, and it's none of your business. You know? I respect you. I could have said I love you, but you've got to keep your nose out of my business and what goes on between me and my wife. These are things that I never did. And, if I were like you, my mother would have never gotten away with the things that she did.
<v Buzz Goldstein>我也跟你实话实说，那是不阳刚的。好的，如果你想谈论不阳刚，那是不阳刚的。我应该采取强硬立场说，看，这是我和我妻子之间的事。我们享受它。它会给我们带来幸福，这不关你的事。你知道吗？我尊重你。我本可以跟我说我爱你，但你必须把你的鼻子从我的事和我妻子之间发生的事里拿开。这些是我从来没做过的事。而且，如果我像你一样，我母亲永远不可能逃脱她所做的那些事。

00:43:43.230 --> 00:43:58.250
<v Jonathan Goldstein>I really didn't expect this. Of course, I've never doubted that my father liked me, but I always felt that it was in spite of what I wasn't and not because of what I was. And what I was was a man who was willing to ignore his own mother.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>我真的没料到会这样。当然，我从来没怀疑过父亲喜欢我，但我总是觉得那是在我“不是什么”的情况下，而不是因为我“是什么”。而我所是的，是一个愿意忽视自己母亲的男人。

00:43:58.250 --> 00:44:30.730
<v Jonathan Goldstein>By the time my father was my age now, he had already served in the Army overseas. He had already entered himself in Golden Glove boxing matches, had two kids, and was working two jobs. And me? When my mother tells me not to put my feet up on the coffee table, I continue to put my feet up on the coffee table. This is what my father is impressed by. This is what makes me a man. I don't want to say this disappoints me, but sometimes I can't help wanting to be manly in exactly the way that he's manly.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>当我父亲现在和我一样大时，他已经去海外服过兵役了。他已经参加过金手套拳击比赛，有了两个孩子，并且做了两份工作。而我呢？当我母亲告诉我不要把脚放在咖啡桌上时，我继续把脚放在咖啡桌上。这就是我父亲印象深刻的地方。这就是让我成为男人的原因。我不想说这让我失望，但有时我不禁想要以他阳刚的方式变得阳刚。

00:44:30.730 --> 00:45:13.160
<v Jonathan Goldstein>When I was a child, my father had chronic back pain, and I was very proud of how loud he could yell in agony. So great was his pain and his rage about his pain, that, one night, he actually yanked the bed board off the bed. After it happened, I would brag to my friends about how strong my father was. From then on, whenever I pulled off some unexpected physical tour de force, like opening a stuck jar of Nutella, my face as red and squinty as a clenched fist, my friends would say that I was pulling out the Goldstein bed board. That's the kind of manly I want to be. That's when I feel at one with my legacy.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>当我还是个孩子时，父亲有长期的背痛，我非常自豪他能因痛苦而喊得有多大声。他的痛苦和对他痛苦的愤怒如此之大，以至于有一天晚上，他实际上把床板从床上扯了下来。事情发生后，我会向朋友们吹嘘我父亲有多强壮。从那时起，每当我做出一些意想不到的身体奇迹时，比如打开一罐卡住的榛子酱，我的脸红得像个紧握的拳头，朋友们就会说我是在拔出“戈德斯坦床板”。那就是我想成为的那种阳刚。那是我感觉与我的遗产合而为一时。

00:45:13.160 --> 00:45:20.370
<v Ira Glass>Jonathan Goldstein is the host of the radio show, Wire Tap, on the CBC, and the author of the novel, Lenny Bruce is Dead.
<v Ira Glass>乔纳森·戈德斯坦是CBC电台节目《Wire Tap》的主持人，也是小说《兰尼·布鲁斯死了》的作者。

00:45:20.370 --> 00:45:22.990
<v Jonathan Goldstein>All right. Talk at the volume you're going to be speaking in.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>好了。用你即将说话的音量说话。

00:45:22.990 --> 00:45:27.170
<v Buzz Goldstein>This is the volume that I'm going to be speaking in.
<v Buzz Goldstein>这是我即将说话的音量。

00:45:27.170 --> 00:45:32.670
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Why don't we start off by giving me a real listen pal.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>我们为什么不从给我一个真实的“听着，伙计”开始呢？

00:45:32.670 --> 00:45:41.610
<v Buzz Goldstein>Listen pal, when I speak, I like people to listen to me and pay attention to what I'm saying. Do you understand what I'm saying? Do you hear me?
<v Buzz Goldstein>听着，伙计，当我说话时，我喜欢人们听我说并注意我在说什么。你明白我在说什么吗？你听到我了吗？

00:45:41.610 --> 00:45:48.570
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Give me a listen pal along with imagining that someone just cut in front of you in line.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>给我一个“听着，伙计”，同时想象有人刚刚插队插在你前面。

00:45:48.570 --> 00:46:00.150
<v Buzz Goldstein>Listen, pal. Do you know there's a line? We've all been waiting here for quite a while. There's a line that goes in the back. You don't start here in the middle or go to the front, you go to the back.
<v Buzz Goldstein>听着，伙计。你知道有排队吗？我们大家都已经在那里等了很久了。有一条排队到后面。你不要从中间或前面开始，你要去后面。

00:46:00.150 --> 00:46:06.860
<v Jonathan Goldstein>All right. Now you have to get a little bit more aggressive because he's not listening to you.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>好了。现在你必须变得更有侵略性一点，因为他没在听你说。

00:46:06.860 --> 00:46:22.230
<v Buzz Goldstein>Hey! What are you, dense? Don't you hear what I'm saying to you? You don't listen? Don't ignore me. Don't ignore me because I'm not going to let you go in front of me. Don't ignore me. That only infuriates me.
<v Buzz Goldstein>嘿！你是笨蛋吗？你没听到我在跟你说什么吗？你没在听吗？不要忽视我。不要忽视我，因为我不会让你走到我前面的。不要忽视我。那只会让我暴怒。

00:46:22.230 --> 00:46:23.940
<v Jonathan Goldstein>And that's the last thing he wants to do.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>而那是他最不想做的事。

00:46:23.940 --> 00:46:32.740
<v Buzz Goldstein>That's right.
<v Buzz Goldstein>没错。

00:46:32.740 --> 00:46:53.210
<v Buzz Goldstein>[MUSIC - "I'M WAITING FOR THE MAN" BY DAVID BOWIE]
<v Buzz Goldstein>[音乐 - 大卫·鲍伊的《I'm Waiting for the Man》]

00:46:53.210 --> 00:47:08.000
<v Ira Glass>Act Three, Heart Shaped Box. Seven years ago, Doug Hill was diagnosed with a rare brain disease called frontal lobe dementia. On our show a while back, we had a story about Doug's son, Nick, who was very young at the time and struggling to understand the disease.
<v Ira Glass>第三幕，“心形盒”。七年前，道格·希尔被诊断出患有一种罕见的脑部疾病，叫额颞叶痴呆。在我们的节目之前，我们有一个关于道格的儿子尼克的报道，尼克当时非常小，正努力理解这种疾病。

00:47:08.000 --> 00:47:25.520
<v Ira Glass>His disease slowly shrinks your brain. Your personality goes, your logic, your emotions. It's pretty brutal, and it's fatal. About a year ago, Doug got much worse and his family started preparing for the end. And, as you might understand, it's been a long, slow, complicated goodbye over the years that he has deteriorated.
<v Ira Glass>他的疾病慢慢萎缩你的大脑。你的个性消失了，你的逻辑，你的情绪。这很残酷，而且是致命的。大约一年前，道格变得更糟了，他的家人开始为终局做准备。而且，你可能理解，这多年来一直是一个漫长、缓慢、复杂的告别，他一直在恶化。

00:47:25.520 --> 00:47:54.280
<v Ira Glass>The therapist said that, for Nick to find closure, for Nick, especially to understand his feelings when his dad finally died, what he was going to need is a picture in his head of who his dad really was back when he was well. Doug needed to be real if Nick was going to be able to let him go. And the job of producing this important biography of this person who Nick, kind of, didn't exactly really know anymore, fell to Nick's mom, Julie.
<v Ira Glass>治疗师说，尼克要找到解脱，尼克要理解他父亲最终去世时的感受，他需要的是在他脑海里有一幅关于他父亲在健康时到底是谁的画面。如果尼克要能够让他离开，道格需要是真实的。而制作这个重要的人物传记的工作——那个尼克某种程度上已经不再真正认识的人——落到了尼克的母亲朱莉身上。

00:47:54.280 --> 00:48:12.920
<v Julie Hill>My son is 10 years old. He's tall, and thin, and blonde, and freckled, and smart, and funny, and generous. In other words, Nick is his father all over again. Yet, Nick knows so little about his dad. He's too young to remember what Doug was like before he got sick.
<v Julie Hill>我儿子10岁。他个子高，瘦，金发，有雀斑，聪明，有趣，慷慨。换句话说，尼克就是他父亲的翻版。然而，尼克对他爸爸了解得太少。他太小了，不记得道格生病前是什么样子。

00:48:12.920 --> 00:48:52.680
<v Julie Hill>His father was far more than the early symptoms of the disease, like Doug constantly repeating himself, or compulsively eating every sweet in sight, or irrationally walking in front of moving traffic. So giving this complete picture of Doug, this hugely important job, falls to me. I'm 43 years old and both my parents are alive. That makes me supremely unqualified, not to mention overwhelmed. I married Doug 20 years ago. Where do I even start? Apparently, with objects.
<v Julie Hill>他的父亲远不止疾病的早期症状，比如道格不断重复自己，或者强迫性地吃掉视线内的每一点甜食，或者非理性地走在移动的交通面前。所以给出道格这幅完整的图画，这个极其重要的工作，落到了我身上。我43岁，我的父母都还活着。这使我完全没有资格，更不用说不知所措。我20年前嫁给了道格。我从哪里开始呢？显然，从物体开始。

00:48:52.680 --> 00:49:23.900
<v Julie Hill>My friend Adrian lost both her parents by the time she was 19. She's told me she still yearns for stuff that her parents touched or carried around, that somehow they connect her to them. So when Doug got sick, Adrian told me to collect simple, everyday items, and that, one day, they'd mean the world to Nick. So early on, Doug's things started making their way into a box, a casket of memories.
<v Julie Hill>我的朋友阿德里安19岁时失去了双亲。她告诉我她仍然渴望父母摸过或随身携带的东西，某种程度上它们将她与他们连接在一起。所以道格生病时，阿德里安告诉我收集简单的日常物品，有一天，它们对尼克来说意义重大。所以很早，道格的东西开始进入一个盒子，一个记忆的棺材。

00:49:23.900 --> 00:49:44.950
<v Julie Hill>The box is about the size of a child's shoe box, and it's made of tin. The remnants of all the colors it once was, red, and green, and yellow, poke through its rusty patina. Long ago, it sat in Doug's grandmother's home in the WASP-y end of Indianapolis, and Doug loved it then as much as Nick does now.
<v Julie Hill>那个盒子大约一个儿童鞋盒大小，是锡制的。它曾经所有的颜色，红色、绿色和黄色，透过它锈迹斑斑的铜绿冒出来。很久以前，它坐在道格在印第安纳波利斯沃斯普区祖母的家里，道格那时对它的热爱就像尼克现在一样。

00:49:44.950 --> 00:50:21.150
<v Julie Hill>Now the box sits on top of a Barrister bookcase in our hallway. It's deliberately up high so that Nick has to ask a grown-up to get it down. Staying slightly out of reach keeps it precious. Plus, this way, the story that the box contains has a ready narrator. There's no master plan for what's been collected: eyeglasses, a press pass, a passport, pictures. Often I've wondered, how could these things be so important?
<v Julie Hill>现在盒子坐在我们走廊的律师书架上。它特意放得很高，这样尼克就必须请一个成年人把它拿下来。保持稍微够不到的位置让它显得珍贵。另外，这样，盒子包含的故事就有一个随时待命的叙述者。收集的东西没有总体计划：眼镜、记者证、护照、照片。我经常想，这些东西怎么会如此重要？

00:50:21.150 --> 00:50:48.530
<v Julie Hill>For the first six years after Doug got sick, Nick didn't want to look inside the box. It scared him. But as his daddy got sicker, on Saturday afternoons I started bringing the box down, combing through the stuff myself. Slowly, Nick would tread to my side. And now he calls the box his treasure. But each time Nick and I go through the box, the discussion is different and surprising, never quite going the way I think it will.
<v Julie Hill>道格生病后的前六年，尼克不想往盒子里看。它吓坏了他。但当他的爸爸病得更重时，周六下午我开始把盒子拿下来，自己梳理里面的东西。慢慢地，尼克会走到我身边。现在他把那个盒子称为他的财宝。但每次尼克和我翻看那个盒子时，讨论都是不同且令人惊讶的，从来没有完全按照我以为的方式进行。

00:50:48.530 --> 00:51:22.940
<v Julie Hill>One of the smaller items in the box is a laminated work ID with CNN's logo. It has Doug's name and the title Video Journalist on it, plus an amazingly handsome picture of him at 22 years old. When Nick pulled it out, it seemed like a great opportunity to make Doug human. I told Nick how his dad was a studio cameraman there, and how, one night, the handle on the camera's wheeled pedestal hooked onto Doug's belt loop as he walked away. Doug's camera was live, so the anchorman and the whole newsdesk had to list to keep up with Doug's rolling shot.
<v Julie Hill>盒子里较小的物品之一是一张带有CNN标志的层压工作证。上面有道格的名字和“视频记者”的头衔，加上一张他22岁时令人惊叹的英俊照片。当尼克把它拿出来时，这似乎是一个让道格变得人性化的绝佳机会。我告诉尼克他爸爸是怎么在那里当录影棚摄影师，以及有一天，摄影机轮式底座上的手柄在道格走开时钩住了他的皮带扣。道格的摄影机是直播的，所以主播和整个新闻台不得不倾斜着以跟上道格的推车镜头。

00:51:22.940 --> 00:51:53.980
<v Julie Hill>Nick laughed a little, but he steered the conversation in a totally different direction. "Did daddy ever work for Fox News?" "No, never." "Oh, thank God. Daddy was a Democrat." And while I thought Nick might want to know his dad was capable of making a goofy mistake, what Nick took away from the work ID was that his daddy leaned left.
<v Julie Hill>尼克笑了笑，但他把谈话引导向了完全不同的方向。“爸爸给福克斯新闻工作过吗？”“没有，从来没有。”“哦，感谢上帝。爸爸是民主党人。”虽然我以为尼克可能想知道他爸爸有能力犯一个愚蠢的错误，但尼克从工作证中带走的是他爸爸偏左。

00:51:53.980 --> 00:52:24.170
<v Julie Hill>Nick loves looking at the pictures, pictures of Doug, especially when he was a kid, when he was 10 in Florida, and when he was 12, sitting at a birthday party with his namesake grandfather. And even more precious are the pictures of Nick and Doug together. There's one of Doug holding our very surprised 18-month-old boy upside down. And one shows Nick as a toddler sitting on his dad's lap as they steer a boat. Yet another shows them sleeping next to each other with Nick curled against his dad.
<v Julie Hill>尼克喜欢看照片，道格的照片，尤其是他小时候，当他在佛罗里达10岁时，以及当他12岁时，坐在生日派对上和同名的爷爷在一起时。更珍贵的是尼克和道格在一起的照片。有一张是道格把我们非常惊讶的18个月大的儿子倒过来抱着。还有一张显示尼克作为一个蹒跚学步的孩子坐在他爸爸膝盖上，他们驾驶着一艘船。还有一张显示他们睡在一起，尼克蜷缩在他爸爸身边。

00:52:24.170 --> 00:52:50.030
<v Julie Hill>The questions come again. "When did that happen?" I try to remember as many details as I can. "We were playing hide-and-seek in the living room when Daddy started swinging you around." "We drove a golf cart to the marina in Florida the day we went out on Uncle Larry's boat. And when you napped together, we were at a beach house near Charleston."
<v Julie Hill>问题再次出现。“那是什么时候发生的？”我尽量记住我能记住的所有细节。“当爸爸开始把你转来转去时，我们正在客厅里玩捉迷藏。”“那天我们坐高尔夫球车去了佛罗里达的码头，我们上了拉里叔叔的船。当我们在一起小睡时，我们在查尔斯顿附近的一个海滩小屋。”

00:52:50.030 --> 00:53:04.240
<v Julie Hill>At the bottom of the box hides Doug's wedding ring. It came off years ago when his hand swelled and the ring cut off circulation. Nick loves to touch it, sliding this metal hula hoop around his number two-pencil fingers.
<v Julie Hill>盒子底部藏着道格的结婚戒指。它是几年前当他的手肿胀，戒指切断循环时摘下来的。尼克喜欢触摸它，把这个金属呼啦圈套在他二号铅笔手指上滑动。

00:53:04.240 --> 00:53:25.600
<v Julie Hill>But some of the objects in the box aren't as popular as others. Nick has never put on Doug's eyeglasses. Cuff links and tuxedo studs sit untouched along with the antique pocket watch I bought Doug for our 10th wedding anniversary. But time and again, it's clear that my friend Adrian was right: sometimes throw-away items have the most meaning.
<v Julie Hill>但盒子里的一些物品不如其他的受欢迎。尼克从来没戴过道格的眼镜。袖扣和燕尾服钉扣和我在我们结婚十周年纪念日给道格买的古董怀表一样，都没被动过。但时而，显而易见，我的朋友阿德里安是对的：有时丢弃的物品最有意义。

00:53:25.600 --> 00:53:51.980
<v Julie Hill>A few months ago, Nick wanted new toys. The policy in our house is he buys toys for himself with his own allowance money. He'd been eyeing some Dungeons & Dragons figures and was short by $10. He was greatly disappointed, not wanting to wait a few more paydays. Then I almost saw the light bulb flash over Nick's head. "I know. I could use the money from Daddy's change jar."
<v Julie Hill>几个月前，尼克想要新玩具。我们家的政策是他用自己的零花钱买玩具。他一直在看一些《龙与地下城》模型，还差10美元。他非常失望，不想再等几个发薪日。然后我几乎看到灯泡在尼克头顶闪亮。“我知道了。我可以用爸爸零钱罐里的钱。”

00:53:51.980 --> 00:54:22.800
<v Julie Hill>When Doug was well, each night he'd come home from work, drop his wallet on the table, and dump his change into a blue ceramic jar. Sometime after Doug moved to the nursing home four years ago, the change jar found a new job: it holds up a family portrait that had lost its easel. This jar has hid on my mantle in plain sight for years, forgotten, at least by me. I pulled it down.
<v Julie Hill>当道格健康时，每天晚上他从工作回家，把钱包丢在桌子上，把零钱倒进一个蓝色陶瓷罐里。道格四年前搬到疗养院后不久，零钱罐找到了新工作：它撑起了一张失去支架的家庭肖像。这个罐子在我壁炉架上明显的地方藏了好几年，被遗忘了，至少对我而言。我把它拿下来。

00:54:22.800 --> 00:54:53.360
<v Julie Hill>For more than an hour, our hands got dirty counting the change that once sat in Doug's pockets. We separated the pennies from the dimes and the quarters from the nickles. And we found things like a New York City subway token, and Canadian pennies, and a receipt for a haircut from 1998. The entire time we talked about his dad, the man I made this beautiful boy with.
<v Julie Hill>超过一个小时，我们的手变得脏兮兮，数着曾经在道格口袋里的零钱。我们把便士和角币分开，四分之一美元和五分镍币分开。我们发现了诸如纽约地铁代币、加拿大便士和1998年的理发收据之类的东西。整个过程中，我们都在谈论他爸爸，那个和我生下这个美丽男孩的男人。

00:54:53.360 --> 00:55:13.390
<v Julie Hill>We counted up more than $50, a fortune to a fourth grader. Nick was amazed. "What would Daddy want me to do with all this money?" "Well, he'd probably want you to save some, then spend some on fun, like those toys you really want." Nick was thrilled, aching for his new toys.
<v Julie Hill>我们数出了超过50美元，对于一个四年级学生来说是一笔财富。尼克很惊讶。“爸爸会希望我用所有这些钱做什么？”“嗯，他可能会希望你存一些，然后花一些在乐趣上，比如那些你真正想要的玩具。”尼克很激动，渴望着他的新玩具。

00:55:13.390 --> 00:56:07.030
<v Julie Hill>On Saturday, we made the trip to the bank and then bought his heart's desire. Back home, Nick ripped open the boxes, joyous for the score, but 10 minutes later he came to me with regretful eyes. "What's wrong, Nick?" He sighed, and swallowed hard, and began to cry. "Daddy's money isn't in the house any more." Pennies, dimes, and nickels, stuff he'd barely bend over to pick up. He felt he'd betrayed his father treating those coins merely as currency. I held him. I tried explaining that these toys, the money in the bank, these were like gifts from his father. But it didn't help. "Those coins were Daddy's, and they'll never be here again."
<v Julie Hill>周六，我们去了银行，然后买了他们的心愿。回到家，尼克撕开了包装盒，为这笔收获感到快乐，但10分钟后他带着后悔的眼睛来到我身边。“怎么了，尼克？”他叹了口气，吞咽得很辛苦，开始哭泣。“爸爸的钱不在房子里了。”便士、角币和镍币，他甚至懒得弯腰去捡的东西。他感到背叛了他的父亲，只是把那些硬币当作货币。我抱着他。我试着解释那些玩具，银行里的钱，这些就像是他父亲的礼物。但这没用。“那些硬币是爸爸的，它们再也不会在这里了。”

00:56:07.030 --> 00:57:03.050
<v Julie Hill>We've lost so much of Doug already. Long ago, we let go of his humor, his writings, his displays of love and kindness. Inch by inch, we've buried him, with each day bringing yet another tiny, unbearable loss. So losing these little things, even just pennies, it's the last straw. It's why the box is so important. It's where these treasures from Doug remain safe for Nick, for Nick, but not for me. I have a different treasure trove of Doug's love, and he stands right next to me. He's tall, and thin, and blonde, and freckled, and smart, and funny, and generous, and he's 10 years old.
<v Julie Hill>我们已经失去了道格的太多东西。很久以前，我们放手了他的幽默、他的写作、他的爱和善良的展示。一寸一寸地，我们埋葬了他，每一天都带来又一个微小、难以承受的损失。所以失去这些小东西，即使只是便士，也是最后一根稻草。这就是盒子如此重要的原因。它是这些来自道格的宝藏对尼克保持安全的地方，对尼克而言，但对我不是。我有另一个道格的爱之宝库，而他就站在我身边。他个子高，瘦，金发，有雀斑，聪明，有趣，慷慨，而他10岁。

00:57:03.050 --> 00:57:43.010
<v Ira Glass>Julie Hill, in Chicago. Three months after she put together this story, Doug finally died.
<v Ira Glass>朱莉·希尔，在芝加哥。她编排这个故事三个月后，道格终于去世了。

00:57:43.010 --> 00:57:51.830
<v Ira Glass>Well, our program was produced today by Diane Cook and myself, with Alex Blumberg, Jane Feltes, Sarah Koenig, and Lisa Pollak. Our senior producer is Julie Snyder. Elizabeth Meister runs our website.
<v Ira Glass>好了，我们的节目今天由黛安·库克和我自己制作，还有亚历克斯·布伦伯格、简·费尔特斯、莎拉·科尼格和丽莎·波拉克。我们的资深制作人是朱莉·斯奈德。伊丽莎白·迈斯特负责我们的网站。

